WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.808 --> 00:00:14.351
PRESENTER: Glad to see the
usual audience at our physics

00:00:14.351 --> 00:00:14.850
colloquium.

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:18.420
[AUDIENCE LAUGHS]

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:22.650
You may think that I'm going
to introduce Wolfgang Ketterle,

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:24.570
but you'd be wrong.

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:28.470
I'm introducing Dan Kleppner.

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:34.260
Dan Kleppner came to MIT
after getting his PhD,

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:36.945
and serving for some years
on the faculty at Harvard.

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:42.750
And he's really famous
for many of the things

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:45.690
he's done in atomic physics.

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:51.060
For Rydberg atoms, making atoms
as large as 1,000 angstroms

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:53.370
in diameter.

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:56.640
For many other things,
and for his work

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:00.840
with Tom Greytak on
Bose condensation,

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:03.550
and spin-aligned hydrogen.

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:07.530
Dan, when he came to MIT,
began the establishment

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:12.090
of what is now a truly great
school of atomic physics.

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:17.190
And the test of a great
school is the students,

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:21.090
and several of those students
have won Nobel Prizes.

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:25.590
Bill Phillips in 1997 was
a student of Dan's, and one

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:28.170
of this year's
prizewinners, Carl Wyman,

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:33.450
did his undergraduate
thesis research with Dan.

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:36.690
Dan is a great physicist,
a great educator.

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:41.700
Those of you who've taken 8012
know his wonderful textbook.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:42.780
He's a great writer.

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:46.860
He writes columns in physics
today that are wonderful.

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:49.080
And he's a great colleague.

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:52.038
And it's my great
pleasure to introduce Dan.

00:01:52.038 --> 00:01:55.524
[APPLAUSE]

00:02:03.492 --> 00:02:08.820
KLEPPNER: So success has many
fathers, and some grandfathers.

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:11.630
And today I am a very
happy grandfather.

00:02:11.630 --> 00:02:13.380
And I suspect that
Norman Ramsey over here

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:15.090
is a very happy
great-grandfather.

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:16.548
[AUDIENCE CHUCKLES]

00:02:16.548 --> 00:02:19.950
[APPLAUSE]

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:28.580
I had the great good fortune
to have Dave Pritchard as one

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:31.460
of my first graduate
students, and to have

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:34.640
the experience of him
as a colleague at MIT

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:36.710
for many years.

00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:38.390
Dave has many accomplishments.

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:39.920
This year he has
mentored, directly,

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:41.730
two of the Nobel Prize winners.

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:44.490
Wolfgang Ketterle,
and Eric Cornell

00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:47.460
was a graduate student of his.

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:49.980
He has made many seminal
contributions to physics.

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:53.010
He invented the
field of atom optics.

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:55.050
And he made seminal
contributions

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:59.280
to the work, which garnered
the Nobel Prize in 1997

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:01.980
for atom cooling, and trapping.

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:04.720
He invented the
Ioffe-Pritchard trap.

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:06.300
He did the seminal
work on what's

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:10.750
called the MOT, which is
the workhorse in that area.

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:15.120
And in 1990 he invited
Wolfgang Ketterle

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:17.960
to come as a post-doc to work
with him on the atom cooling,

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:21.500
even though Wolfgang had
no background in that area.

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:25.050
He spotted Wolfgang's
qualities, and the two of them

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:28.680
together did such fabulous work
that it was clear that Wolfgang

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:30.720
was headed for great things.

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:34.950
But not at MIT, because we could
not appoint a young faculty

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:38.150
member who would
be in competition

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:40.640
with a senior faculty
member, or who would really

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:41.930
be in close collaboration.

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:46.010
Because we expect our faculty to
be able to work independently.

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:49.040
Dave overcame that obstacle
by withdrawing from the field,

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:52.040
and turning over his
equipment to Wolfgang.

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:55.400
So Wolfgang could start out his
work towards Bose condensation

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:57.490
at top speed.

00:03:57.490 --> 00:04:00.880
And the rest of that is history,
and you'll hear more about it.

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:04.272
But to me this
example of mentoring,

00:04:04.272 --> 00:04:05.980
which is to leave a
field in which you're

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:08.620
very interested in doing
outstanding work to make room

00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:13.330
for young talent, is in the
very best tradition of science

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:15.010
and of teaching.

00:04:15.010 --> 00:04:18.490
And I hope in the very
best tradition of MIT.

00:04:18.490 --> 00:04:20.890
So with this those
thoughts, I would like now

00:04:20.890 --> 00:04:23.018
to introduce to
you, Dave Pritchard.

00:04:23.018 --> 00:04:26.434
[APPLAUSE]

00:04:41.074 --> 00:04:44.460
PRITCHARD: Well, thank
you very much Dan.

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:46.700
By my account, this
is the fourth time

00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:49.640
that Wolfgang has given an
MIT physics colloquium here

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:53.430
in the six years since
he's been on the faculty,

00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:55.430
and up to the seventh now.

00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:59.030
This is a sign that, well, it's
actually in the past six years.

00:04:59.030 --> 00:05:02.390
This is a sign that MIT
recognized his worth somewhat

00:05:02.390 --> 00:05:06.320
before the Nobel
Prize committee.

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:09.264
Since I've had the honor of
introducing him three times,

00:05:09.264 --> 00:05:11.930
I thought I might just summarize
those introductions to give you

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:13.310
an idea of his growth.

00:05:13.310 --> 00:05:15.393
Now I know some of you
were saying, wait a minute,

00:05:15.393 --> 00:05:18.930
three introducers, and
now three introductions.

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:21.180
But it's okay because
we discussed it.

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:23.180
And we realized that
if each one of us

00:05:23.180 --> 00:05:26.089
talked a factor of three
longer than the first, then

00:05:26.089 --> 00:05:28.130
the previous speaker, and
Wolfgang followed suit,

00:05:28.130 --> 00:05:31.714
he'd still talk for more
than 2/3 of the time.

00:05:31.714 --> 00:05:32.630
You can work that out.

00:05:35.670 --> 00:05:39.519
So in the first introduction I
said, Wolfgang was my post-doc,

00:05:39.519 --> 00:05:41.060
and we were fortunate
to get him here

00:05:41.060 --> 00:05:44.630
to take over running
my cold atom apparatus

00:05:44.630 --> 00:05:47.340
because he can run it so
much better than I could.

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:49.910
As expected, he has
made fantastic progress.

00:05:49.910 --> 00:05:53.210
Now having made an
observation of BEC,

00:05:53.210 --> 00:05:56.210
but with orders of magnitude
more atoms than the condensate

00:05:56.210 --> 00:05:59.670
that Eric Cornell made at JILA.

00:05:59.670 --> 00:06:03.870
And the second introduction
not too much, but a year later

00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:07.440
I said, Wolfgang is
absolutely courageous.

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:10.520
With the best BEC, the
biggest BEC in the world,

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:13.830
and with his phone ringing
off the hook with invitations.

00:06:13.830 --> 00:06:16.200
Neither of these is
an irrelevant concern

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:19.080
for a professor who
doesn't have tenure.

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:21.930
He decided to scrap
his magnetic trap.

00:06:21.930 --> 00:06:25.322
He felt that a new cloverleaf
magnetic trap that he designed

00:06:25.322 --> 00:06:27.030
would allow for better
scientific studies

00:06:27.030 --> 00:06:28.810
of the condensate.

00:06:28.810 --> 00:06:30.540
As usual, he was
right, and so on.

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:31.480
So you want to hear
something like that.

00:06:31.480 --> 00:06:32.396
That's the second one.

00:06:32.396 --> 00:06:36.740
The third time I said, Wolfgang
Ketterle needs no introduction.

00:06:36.740 --> 00:06:37.240
He--

00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:38.200
[AUDIENCE LAUGHS]

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:41.536
[CHUCKLES]

00:06:41.536 --> 00:06:43.410
He was just promoted
from assistant professor

00:06:43.410 --> 00:06:45.420
to a full professor
in a single jump,

00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:48.240
and he justifies this by doing
spectacular research, and so

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:50.530
on, and so forth.

00:06:50.530 --> 00:06:52.890
Now when I hear the trite
phrase, needs no introduction,

00:06:52.890 --> 00:06:54.900
I often know the speaker myself.

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:57.450
And I then agree, of
course, with the introducer.

00:06:57.450 --> 00:07:00.120
But it always raises in
my mind the question, well

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:02.220
who's this guy who's
doing the introduction?

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:04.170
Doesn't he need an introduction?

00:07:04.170 --> 00:07:06.180
And at this fourth
introduction I

00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:10.350
see that the relative
stature of Wolfgang

00:07:10.350 --> 00:07:13.440
has grown so disproportionately
that many other people are now

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:15.060
concerned with this issue.

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:16.905
And that obviously
explains today's format

00:07:16.905 --> 00:07:18.738
where there were two
guys who introduced me.

00:07:18.738 --> 00:07:22.292
[AUDIENCE LAUGHS]

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:26.310
Occasionally I
feel that I should

00:07:26.310 --> 00:07:29.150
try to retain some
of my mentorship.

00:07:29.150 --> 00:07:31.740
A vestige of that, and
so I try to challenge

00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:34.050
Wolfgang to get even better.

00:07:34.050 --> 00:07:37.080
This is really difficult to
do, because of all the people

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:39.750
that I've worked with,
which so far includes

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:41.340
six Nobel laureates.

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:45.840
He's the only one who was
A-plus at absolutely everything,

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:49.110
scientific tastes,
design, organization,

00:07:49.110 --> 00:07:52.620
group management,
writing, scientific talks,

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:55.380
public lectures, A-plus.

00:07:55.380 --> 00:07:57.254
So challenging them
is like saying,

00:07:57.254 --> 00:07:59.670
well very good about jumping
over all those tall buildings

00:07:59.670 --> 00:08:01.500
with a single bound,
but if you could

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:02.970
learn to jump over
whole cities you

00:08:02.970 --> 00:08:05.850
wouldn't crush so
many automobiles.

00:08:05.850 --> 00:08:07.500
Now previously
I've challenged him

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:10.620
by guaranteeing that he would
give a spectacular talk.

00:08:10.620 --> 00:08:12.930
And actually today I
can raise the bar simply

00:08:12.930 --> 00:08:15.704
by challenging him
to do that again,

00:08:15.704 --> 00:08:17.370
because I know that
this is a week where

00:08:17.370 --> 00:08:18.510
he hasn't had much sleep.

00:08:18.510 --> 00:08:21.570
And he's had numerous
distractions from the press,

00:08:21.570 --> 00:08:23.880
and from the many interviews.

00:08:23.880 --> 00:08:26.370
But I'm sure that he's up to it.

00:08:26.370 --> 00:08:31.140
One final note, just from
my personal perspective.

00:08:31.140 --> 00:08:35.789
I want to say that your most
important, A-plus ability is

00:08:35.789 --> 00:08:37.440
as a colleague and friend.

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:39.870
My world has been
tremendously brightened

00:08:39.870 --> 00:08:41.220
since you walked into my lab.

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:44.156
And I'm glad to see that
MIT is the same way.

00:08:44.156 --> 00:08:47.635
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:15.964 --> 00:09:18.449
[SIGHS]

00:09:18.449 --> 00:09:21.200
KETTERLE: What a place,
and what an introduction.

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:24.320
I think you heard from the
previous people who introduced

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:27.710
me what nice a place this is.

00:09:27.710 --> 00:09:30.710
What great mentors I've
had, and sometimes I

00:09:30.710 --> 00:09:35.750
feel I was privileged to finish
what those people have started.

00:09:35.750 --> 00:09:38.150
And you will see
that in my talk.

00:09:38.150 --> 00:09:40.590
But you, I think you've also
heard from those introduction

00:09:40.590 --> 00:09:44.440
that this place, and
the atomic physics

00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:46.670
community, or the
physics department,

00:09:46.670 --> 00:09:49.780
is an inspiring and
friendly environment.

00:09:49.780 --> 00:09:54.190
And it is this environment
which brings the best out of us.

00:09:54.190 --> 00:09:56.560
Now Dave was raising
the bar by saying,

00:09:56.560 --> 00:09:59.320
by sort of announcing
an A-plus talk.

00:09:59.320 --> 00:10:03.370
I think this talk for
me has two superlatives.

00:10:03.370 --> 00:10:06.160
Number one, it is the
best-attended lecture

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:06.860
I've ever given.

00:10:06.860 --> 00:10:08.631
[AUDIENCE LAUGHS]

00:10:08.631 --> 00:10:09.130
[HUFFS]

00:10:09.130 --> 00:10:11.470
But now I'm embarrassed
to say number two.

00:10:11.470 --> 00:10:13.720
It's a worse prepared one.

00:10:13.720 --> 00:10:16.580
Because I was given very
short notice, and I thought,

00:10:16.580 --> 00:10:18.850
so well, I'll just put a
few note cards together.

00:10:18.850 --> 00:10:21.040
But the closer this
talk came, the more

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:24.040
I felt it's a very
emotional talk for me.

00:10:24.040 --> 00:10:25.710
Because this is my family.

00:10:25.710 --> 00:10:27.070
This is my place.

00:10:27.070 --> 00:10:29.830
And I wanted to tell a
story, and not just give

00:10:29.830 --> 00:10:31.450
a talk on our recent results.

00:10:31.450 --> 00:10:34.170
So I try to prepare
something special,

00:10:34.170 --> 00:10:36.040
but I couldn't really polish it.

00:10:36.040 --> 00:10:40.540
So let me tell this story of
Bose-Einstein condensates,

00:10:40.540 --> 00:10:44.800
which is the coldest
matter in the universe.

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:47.170
And since I know here
is a part audience,

00:10:47.170 --> 00:10:52.310
I want to be explicit
in one of the concepts.

00:10:52.310 --> 00:10:55.150
So the ball's condensate,
you can regard it

00:10:55.150 --> 00:10:57.520
as metal made of matter waves.

00:10:57.520 --> 00:11:00.850
It's a form of matter where
the quantum mechanical nature,

00:11:00.850 --> 00:11:04.060
the wave nature of
matter, manifests itself

00:11:04.060 --> 00:11:06.559
at a microscopic scale.

00:11:06.559 --> 00:11:10.510
So you're all familiar
with the, or most of you

00:11:10.510 --> 00:11:14.860
I hope, with a particle-wave
duality for light, for photons.

00:11:14.860 --> 00:11:17.380
But we have the same
particle-wave duality

00:11:17.380 --> 00:11:18.820
for all particles.

00:11:18.820 --> 00:11:21.400
We know from the early
days of quantum mechanics

00:11:21.400 --> 00:11:25.060
that a particle, which is
classically characterized

00:11:25.060 --> 00:11:28.180
by position and
velocity, should either

00:11:28.180 --> 00:11:31.930
be described as a quantum
mechanical wave, a de Broglie

00:11:31.930 --> 00:11:35.020
wave, and the wavelengths
of the de Broglie

00:11:35.020 --> 00:11:37.510
wave of the quantum
mechanical wave

00:11:37.510 --> 00:11:39.850
follows the famous
de Broglie relation.

00:11:39.850 --> 00:11:44.260
It's inversely proportional to
the velocity of the particle.

00:11:44.260 --> 00:11:46.810
And I know when
we learn physics,

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:48.400
we all struggled
with the concept

00:11:48.400 --> 00:11:51.700
that particles are all objects.

00:11:51.700 --> 00:11:53.650
You, and me, and
microscopic objects,

00:11:53.650 --> 00:11:56.110
and macroscopic objects
are waves and particles

00:11:56.110 --> 00:11:57.900
at the same time.

00:11:57.900 --> 00:12:01.140
Particles propagate as waves,
but when we detect them

00:12:01.140 --> 00:12:03.780
it makes click in a detector.

00:12:03.780 --> 00:12:07.170
So well, if you and
me feel all waves,

00:12:07.170 --> 00:12:11.690
why don't we perceive
the wave nature of matter

00:12:11.690 --> 00:12:13.830
in everyday life?

00:12:13.830 --> 00:12:16.070
So let me raise you, let
me raise the question,

00:12:16.070 --> 00:12:19.250
how do we perceive if
something is a wave or not?

00:12:19.250 --> 00:12:20.750
So if I would
challenge you and say,

00:12:20.750 --> 00:12:23.940
explain me why sound is a wave.

00:12:23.940 --> 00:12:26.300
A very reasonable
answer would be,

00:12:26.300 --> 00:12:28.730
if there are two people
who talk to each other,

00:12:28.730 --> 00:12:32.020
they can hear each other even
if they don't see each other.

00:12:32.020 --> 00:12:36.420
Because the wave propagates,
deflects around corners.

00:12:38.960 --> 00:12:42.350
If I would ask you, what
happens in the case of light?

00:12:42.350 --> 00:12:44.600
You all know that light is
an electromagnetic wave.

00:12:44.600 --> 00:12:47.780
It's not so obvious, because
the wavelengths of light

00:12:47.780 --> 00:12:51.680
is so short that light is
just deflecting a little bit.

00:12:51.680 --> 00:12:54.140
And you can't really perceive
the wave nature of light

00:12:54.140 --> 00:12:56.200
very easily.

00:12:56.200 --> 00:12:58.360
But now in the case
of matter waves,

00:12:58.360 --> 00:13:00.570
the wavelengths
are even shorter.

00:13:00.570 --> 00:13:04.060
The wavelengths is given
by the de Broglie relation,

00:13:04.060 --> 00:13:05.950
and the velocity
of a particle is

00:13:05.950 --> 00:13:08.340
determined by its temperature.

00:13:08.340 --> 00:13:12.110
So from that we know that
the colder the temperature,

00:13:12.110 --> 00:13:15.200
the longer is the de
Broglie wavelengths.

00:13:15.200 --> 00:13:18.770
Now talking about atoms,
at room temperature,

00:13:18.770 --> 00:13:21.800
the matter wavelengths,
the de Broglie wavelengths,

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:24.510
is smaller than the
size of an atom.

00:13:24.510 --> 00:13:29.380
So therefore the wave nature
of atoms is not manifest.

00:13:29.380 --> 00:13:32.470
But now we can cool down,
and the title of my talk

00:13:32.470 --> 00:13:34.930
is, The Coldest Matter
in the Universe.

00:13:34.930 --> 00:13:38.530
We cool down to micro and
nano Kelvin temperatures.

00:13:38.530 --> 00:13:41.950
More than a million times
colder than interstellar space.

00:13:41.950 --> 00:13:45.290
And the de Broglie wavelengths
becomes longer and longer.

00:13:45.290 --> 00:13:50.260
And this value is
30 micrometer is

00:13:50.260 --> 00:13:52.280
a fraction of the
diameter of a human hair.

00:13:52.280 --> 00:13:54.450
So it's really
getting microscopic.

00:13:54.450 --> 00:13:56.780
And this long de
Broglie wavelengths,

00:13:56.780 --> 00:13:59.260
they are at the heart
of the phenomenon which

00:13:59.260 --> 00:14:00.560
I want to describe.

00:14:00.560 --> 00:14:02.960
Bose-Einstein condensation.

00:14:02.960 --> 00:14:06.110
What happens if you take
a gas in a container,

00:14:06.110 --> 00:14:07.520
and cool it down?

00:14:07.520 --> 00:14:09.986
The particles slow down.

00:14:09.986 --> 00:14:11.360
As I just mentioned,
we shouldn't

00:14:11.360 --> 00:14:14.464
describe the particles as
sort of little billiard balls.

00:14:14.464 --> 00:14:15.380
They are wave packets.

00:14:15.380 --> 00:14:17.690
They are quantum
mechanical waves.

00:14:17.690 --> 00:14:20.410
But as long as the
wavelengths is very short,

00:14:20.410 --> 00:14:22.910
those wave packets
are very localized.

00:14:22.910 --> 00:14:26.420
And we can follow the
motion of those wave packets

00:14:26.420 --> 00:14:28.580
as if they were particles.

00:14:28.580 --> 00:14:30.120
But now we can cool down.

00:14:30.120 --> 00:14:32.900
The de Broglie wavelengths
becomes longer and longer.

00:14:32.900 --> 00:14:36.040
And when we reach the point
where the wave packets,

00:14:36.040 --> 00:14:38.420
where the de Broglie
waves overlap,

00:14:38.420 --> 00:14:41.330
then we cannot follow
individual particles anymore.

00:14:41.330 --> 00:14:46.710
They become sort of a
quantum soup of wave packets,

00:14:46.710 --> 00:14:49.900
and it's exactly at this point
when the wavelengths, the de

00:14:49.900 --> 00:14:51.900
Broglie wavelengths is
comparable to the spacing

00:14:51.900 --> 00:14:55.080
of particles that a new
form of matter forms.

00:14:55.080 --> 00:14:58.840
And this is the
Bose-Einstein condensate.

00:14:58.840 --> 00:15:01.930
Or to say it again, if the
de Broglie wavelengths become

00:15:01.930 --> 00:15:05.890
longer and longer, and those
matter waves overlap, then

00:15:05.890 --> 00:15:08.050
all the particles
in the gas, they

00:15:08.050 --> 00:15:10.630
start to oscillate in concert.

00:15:10.630 --> 00:15:13.540
And what they form is
one giant matter wave.

00:15:13.540 --> 00:15:17.880
And this is the
Bose-Einstein condensates.

00:15:17.880 --> 00:15:19.830
Just use an analogy.

00:15:19.830 --> 00:15:23.610
The difference between
atoms in random motion,

00:15:23.610 --> 00:15:27.750
and the Bose condensate is
exactly the same difference

00:15:27.750 --> 00:15:30.030
as the light from a
light bulb, and the light

00:15:30.030 --> 00:15:31.620
coming out of a laser.

00:15:31.620 --> 00:15:34.060
So what Bose-Einstein
condensate is about,

00:15:34.060 --> 00:15:39.380
it's about the creation of atoms
with laser light properties.

00:15:39.380 --> 00:15:41.300
And this is a nice
artist's conception

00:15:41.300 --> 00:15:44.150
of how you can imagine the
Bose-Einstein condensate.

00:15:44.150 --> 00:15:48.170
First of all, these are
indistinguishable atoms.

00:15:48.170 --> 00:15:50.660
You see they have all the
same facial expression,

00:15:50.660 --> 00:15:54.440
and they march in lockstep,
which means they are in phase.

00:15:54.440 --> 00:15:56.117
It's a single wave function.

00:15:56.117 --> 00:15:57.950
And this is how you can
imagine what happens

00:15:57.950 --> 00:15:59.750
in a Bose-Einstein condensate.

00:15:59.750 --> 00:16:02.990
You also see sort
of those other guys.

00:16:02.990 --> 00:16:04.770
They walk in random direction.

00:16:04.770 --> 00:16:06.010
They're not Bose condensed.

00:16:06.010 --> 00:16:07.790
They are in different
quantum states,

00:16:07.790 --> 00:16:10.250
and are therefore
distinguishable from the atoms

00:16:10.250 --> 00:16:12.330
in the condensate.

00:16:12.330 --> 00:16:15.540
Now of course, what I just said,
that all the atoms go into one

00:16:15.540 --> 00:16:17.580
quantum state, and
fall in lockstep

00:16:17.580 --> 00:16:22.800
is only possible for atoms
which we'll refer to as bosons.

00:16:22.800 --> 00:16:25.969
Particles with
have integer spin.

00:16:25.969 --> 00:16:27.510
I don't want to go
into details here,

00:16:27.510 --> 00:16:31.270
but there is another class
of particles called fermions.

00:16:31.270 --> 00:16:34.230
The have half integer
spin, and their behavior

00:16:34.230 --> 00:16:37.210
at low temperature is
completely different.

00:16:37.210 --> 00:16:39.390
So the phenomenon of
Bose-Einstein condensation

00:16:39.390 --> 00:16:42.780
can be regarded as the
most striking manifestation

00:16:42.780 --> 00:16:45.940
of quantum statistics, of
the difference between bosons

00:16:45.940 --> 00:16:48.300
and fermions.

00:16:48.300 --> 00:16:50.220
And the name
Bose-Einstein condensation

00:16:50.220 --> 00:16:52.590
is still alive from those
two well-known people

00:16:52.590 --> 00:16:59.000
who predicted it in the 1920s.

00:16:59.000 --> 00:17:01.580
However, Bose-Einstein
condensation in a gas

00:17:01.580 --> 00:17:05.420
was not realized until
'95 for reasons which will

00:17:05.420 --> 00:17:07.220
become obvious in a moment.

00:17:07.220 --> 00:17:11.180
So the prediction of
Bose-Einstein's statistics

00:17:11.180 --> 00:17:12.740
was 1925.

00:17:12.740 --> 00:17:16.010
And for many, many years,
the only manifestation

00:17:16.010 --> 00:17:19.130
of Bose-Einstein condensation
was in liquid helium.

00:17:19.130 --> 00:17:21.349
The superfluity of
liquid helium is

00:17:21.349 --> 00:17:23.839
a consequence of
Bose-Einstein condensation,

00:17:23.839 --> 00:17:25.190
but it's a liquid.

00:17:25.190 --> 00:17:26.000
It's not a gas.

00:17:26.000 --> 00:17:27.890
And there are high
density effects, which

00:17:27.890 --> 00:17:31.760
modify this phenomenon greatly.

00:17:31.760 --> 00:17:34.430
But then there was
an attempt to realize

00:17:34.430 --> 00:17:36.670
Bose-Einstein
condensation dilute gases,

00:17:36.670 --> 00:17:40.970
and a lot of credit for that
should go to Tom Greytak,

00:17:40.970 --> 00:17:43.500
and Dan Kleppner, who
are here on the faculty.

00:17:43.500 --> 00:17:46.430
They really put
Bose-Einstein condensation,

00:17:46.430 --> 00:17:50.810
and dilute atomic gases on the
agenda, and made it a goal.

00:17:50.810 --> 00:17:53.310
And it's, I'm very
pleased to mention

00:17:53.310 --> 00:17:56.130
that after 20 years
of efforts they

00:17:56.130 --> 00:17:58.770
realized Bose-Einstein
condensation in hydrogen,

00:17:58.770 --> 00:18:02.720
and achieved a
longstanding goal.

00:18:02.720 --> 00:18:04.940
However, a few
years earlier there

00:18:04.940 --> 00:18:06.620
were new cooling
techniques developed.

00:18:06.620 --> 00:18:09.020
Laser cooling, I would
briefly comment on them.

00:18:09.020 --> 00:18:12.220
And new systems, alkali
atoms, could be cooled

00:18:12.220 --> 00:18:13.910
to ultra low temperatures.

00:18:13.910 --> 00:18:16.250
And it's really the
alkali atoms which

00:18:16.250 --> 00:18:18.140
have created this
whole new field

00:18:18.140 --> 00:18:21.920
of Bose-Einstein condensation
with many, many activities.

00:18:21.920 --> 00:18:25.700
And the success
here came in '95.

00:18:25.700 --> 00:18:29.240
And I will tell you
the story today.

00:18:29.240 --> 00:18:31.750
So well, I think from
most of the concepts

00:18:31.750 --> 00:18:33.850
I've presented, if
you want to achieve

00:18:33.850 --> 00:18:36.900
Bose-Einstein condensation,
quote, unquote.

00:18:36.900 --> 00:18:38.970
All you have to do is
you have to cool down

00:18:38.970 --> 00:18:41.780
the gas to micro and
nano Kelvin temperatures.

00:18:41.780 --> 00:18:45.720
You have to cool it down until
the de Broglie wavelengths is

00:18:45.720 --> 00:18:47.940
comparable to the
spacing between atoms,

00:18:47.940 --> 00:18:49.950
and the matter waves
overlap, and start

00:18:49.950 --> 00:18:52.170
to oscillate in concert.

00:18:52.170 --> 00:18:54.630
So, I mention that the
de Broglie wavelengths

00:18:54.630 --> 00:18:56.200
depends on temperature.

00:18:56.200 --> 00:18:58.980
The distance between
atoms depends on density.

00:18:58.980 --> 00:19:02.670
So we haven't, so the transition
to Bose-Einstein condensation

00:19:02.670 --> 00:19:06.210
is characterized by a
combination of temperature

00:19:06.210 --> 00:19:07.900
and density.

00:19:07.900 --> 00:19:10.690
So let's just look at this
relation for something

00:19:10.690 --> 00:19:12.730
of the density of water.

00:19:12.730 --> 00:19:15.650
It would predict that the
transition temperature is 1

00:19:15.650 --> 00:19:18.270
Kelvin, which is easy to reach.

00:19:18.270 --> 00:19:22.510
However, tough luck at 1 Kelvin
everything is liquid or frozen,

00:19:22.510 --> 00:19:24.610
and you can't observe the
phenomenon Bose-Einstein

00:19:24.610 --> 00:19:26.270
condensation.

00:19:26.270 --> 00:19:29.150
So, in order to
prevent that, and also

00:19:29.150 --> 00:19:31.370
to prevent formation
of molecules

00:19:31.370 --> 00:19:35.870
out of the atomic gas, we had to
work at extremely low density.

00:19:35.870 --> 00:19:37.880
So the system I'm
referring to is

00:19:37.880 --> 00:19:42.180
at a density which is a billion
times more dilute than water.

00:19:42.180 --> 00:19:45.720
It's 100,000 times
thinner than air.

00:19:45.720 --> 00:19:47.910
And in those gases,
we can observe

00:19:47.910 --> 00:19:51.580
Bose-Einstein condensation
without self-destruction

00:19:51.580 --> 00:19:55.340
by molecule cluster
formation, and solidification.

00:19:55.340 --> 00:19:57.020
But there is a price to be paid.

00:19:57.020 --> 00:19:59.090
If you work at
ultra low density,

00:19:59.090 --> 00:20:01.460
you need ultra long
de Broglie wavelengths

00:20:01.460 --> 00:20:04.410
to make those matter waves
oscillate in concept.

00:20:04.410 --> 00:20:07.430
So we needed the lowest
temperatures ever achieved.

00:20:10.220 --> 00:20:12.100
So we needed cooling methods.

00:20:12.100 --> 00:20:13.780
And what I'm actually
talking about

00:20:13.780 --> 00:20:15.840
is a combination of
two different cooling

00:20:15.840 --> 00:20:20.460
methods, which we had developed
in two different subareas.

00:20:20.460 --> 00:20:23.190
One is laser cooling.

00:20:23.190 --> 00:20:24.835
the pioneers of laser cooling.

00:20:28.510 --> 00:20:32.410
Some of them were recognized
with a Nobel Prize in '97.

00:20:32.410 --> 00:20:36.830
And major developments
to laser cooling.

00:20:36.830 --> 00:20:39.670
Were also done by
Dave Pritchard at MIT.

00:20:39.670 --> 00:20:42.260
The principle of laser
cooling is fairly simple.

00:20:42.260 --> 00:20:45.440
You shine laser light on
atoms, the atom skate on light.

00:20:45.440 --> 00:20:47.690
And if you play some tricks,
which I don't want to you

00:20:47.690 --> 00:20:52.420
explain, that the light, which
is scattered, which is emitted,

00:20:52.420 --> 00:20:54.160
has a shorter
wavelength, is more

00:20:54.160 --> 00:20:56.470
energetic than the
absorbed light,

00:20:56.470 --> 00:21:00.010
than the scattering of light
removes energy from the system,

00:21:00.010 --> 00:21:02.180
and the system cools down.

00:21:02.180 --> 00:21:03.130
It works great.

00:21:03.130 --> 00:21:05.080
You just shine laser
light on atoms,

00:21:05.080 --> 00:21:08.220
and you create
micro-Kelvin samples.

00:21:08.220 --> 00:21:11.500
And a very important
configuration to do that

00:21:11.500 --> 00:21:14.730
is the one co-invented
by Dave Pritchard.

00:21:14.730 --> 00:21:18.960
And I think this is probably one
of the most important singular

00:21:18.960 --> 00:21:20.970
techniques which
were ever introduced

00:21:20.970 --> 00:21:23.820
to the field of atomic physics,
the magneto-optical trap.

00:21:23.820 --> 00:21:28.720
It is the workhorse of the whole
field of ultra cold atoms now.

00:21:28.720 --> 00:21:31.990
So well, and if you have this
configuration of laser light,

00:21:31.990 --> 00:21:35.980
and shine light into a vacuum
chamber, and add some atoms.

00:21:35.980 --> 00:21:39.070
You see, this is in one
of our lab upstairs.

00:21:39.070 --> 00:21:42.540
You see a small ball, a few
millimeters in diameter,

00:21:42.540 --> 00:21:44.260
yellow glow.

00:21:44.260 --> 00:21:48.220
So you can see with the naked
eye, ultra cold sodium atoms.

00:21:48.220 --> 00:21:49.930
But at the fairly
hot temperature

00:21:49.930 --> 00:21:52.460
of a fraction of a milli-Kelvin.

00:21:52.460 --> 00:21:54.820
So this is the starting
point for the next stage

00:21:54.820 --> 00:21:58.560
of cooling towards
Bose-Einstein condensation.

00:21:58.560 --> 00:22:00.360
Laser cooling has
so far not been

00:22:00.360 --> 00:22:03.930
able to carry atoms down to
Bose-Einstein condensation,

00:22:03.930 --> 00:22:05.790
because it has limitations.

00:22:05.790 --> 00:22:07.980
And some of them are
very fundamental.

00:22:07.980 --> 00:22:11.930
If you scatter light from
atoms, each photon is the,

00:22:11.930 --> 00:22:13.020
photons are grainy.

00:22:13.020 --> 00:22:14.670
It's like sort of
sand corns which

00:22:14.670 --> 00:22:18.540
transfer a finite of equal
momentum to the atoms.

00:22:18.540 --> 00:22:23.430
So therefore, the temperature
of the standard laser cooling

00:22:23.430 --> 00:22:26.500
methods is limited
to micro-Kelvin.

00:22:26.500 --> 00:22:28.145
So well, if you
can't get very cold

00:22:28.145 --> 00:22:29.770
you should get very
dense, because it's

00:22:29.770 --> 00:22:31.690
a density-temperature
combination which makes

00:22:31.690 --> 00:22:33.340
Bose condensation happens.

00:22:33.340 --> 00:22:36.280
But unfortunately, there's
also a density limitation

00:22:36.280 --> 00:22:39.370
in laser cooling, which,
without going into details,

00:22:39.370 --> 00:22:42.880
is simply set by the process.

00:22:42.880 --> 00:22:45.130
If you have a very dense
sample, all the laser light

00:22:45.130 --> 00:22:47.980
is absorbed, and laser
cooling doesn't work.

00:22:47.980 --> 00:22:52.120
So laser cooling, at least
in the beginning of the 90s,

00:22:52.120 --> 00:22:55.950
fell short by four orders
of magnitude in density,

00:22:55.950 --> 00:22:58.210
or three orders of
magnitude in temperature,

00:22:58.210 --> 00:23:01.400
to reach Bose-Einstein
condensation.

00:23:01.400 --> 00:23:04.670
So well, this is something
which Dave Pritchard developed.

00:23:07.230 --> 00:23:09.160
And I was a post-doc with him.

00:23:09.160 --> 00:23:12.030
But next door is
Dan Kleppner, who

00:23:12.030 --> 00:23:15.570
together with Tom
Greytak, and Harold Hess,

00:23:15.570 --> 00:23:17.400
developed evaporative cooling.

00:23:17.400 --> 00:23:19.260
And this is a different
cooling method,

00:23:19.260 --> 00:23:22.980
which is not suffering from
all the limitations of laser

00:23:22.980 --> 00:23:23.790
cooling.

00:23:23.790 --> 00:23:26.730
So let me explain what
evaporative cooling is.

00:23:26.730 --> 00:23:28.860
Actually, before I
explain the slide,

00:23:28.860 --> 00:23:32.110
evaporative cooling is what
happens in everyday life.

00:23:32.110 --> 00:23:35.280
If you sit in a bathtub,
the water cools down.

00:23:35.280 --> 00:23:39.180
And it cools down because
the hottest particles

00:23:39.180 --> 00:23:40.710
escape as steam.

00:23:40.710 --> 00:23:44.100
And what remains behind are
the less energetic particles.

00:23:44.100 --> 00:23:46.540
This is called evaporation.

00:23:46.540 --> 00:23:48.540
So well, if you do
it in an atom trap,

00:23:48.540 --> 00:23:52.070
if you can find atoms
with magnetic forces,

00:23:52.070 --> 00:23:54.160
we create such a potential.

00:23:54.160 --> 00:23:57.150
It's like an invisible
container, the walls

00:23:57.150 --> 00:23:59.490
of which are magnetic fields.

00:23:59.490 --> 00:24:01.710
I don't want to say
more about it right now.

00:24:01.710 --> 00:24:03.960
But so if particles
in this container,

00:24:03.960 --> 00:24:06.270
and if two such
particles collide,

00:24:06.270 --> 00:24:09.674
one can lower its
energy, come to rest.

00:24:09.674 --> 00:24:11.340
These are the particles
we want to keep.

00:24:11.340 --> 00:24:12.510
They're really cold.

00:24:12.510 --> 00:24:15.360
And the more energetic
one can escape.

00:24:15.360 --> 00:24:17.850
And this was suggested
by Harold Hess,

00:24:17.850 --> 00:24:24.240
and realized in the group of
Dan Kleppner and Tom Greytak.

00:24:24.240 --> 00:24:28.290
So if we have a method to
kind of eliminate the hottest

00:24:28.290 --> 00:24:32.100
particles in many, many steps,
and the remaining particles

00:24:32.100 --> 00:24:33.280
we thermalize.

00:24:33.280 --> 00:24:36.720
We create a thermal
distribution which gets

00:24:36.720 --> 00:24:39.970
colder, and colder, and colder.

00:24:39.970 --> 00:24:43.390
So the question is
how to implement it?

00:24:43.390 --> 00:24:47.040
And Dave Pritchard
had a brilliant idea.

00:24:47.040 --> 00:24:51.330
We sort of X which,
so well, it's even

00:24:51.330 --> 00:24:54.180
dripping with blood here.

00:24:54.180 --> 00:24:57.200
Okay, well what is a
suitable X for atoms?

00:24:57.200 --> 00:25:00.690
So well, if you're in a magnetic
trap, there's a very simple X.

00:25:00.690 --> 00:25:04.590
And this is just switch the
magnetic moment of the atoms

00:25:04.590 --> 00:25:07.560
so that the attractive
force, the confinement force

00:25:07.560 --> 00:25:09.210
turns into a repulsive one.

00:25:09.210 --> 00:25:12.090
You just have to shine a radio
frequency onto the atoms,

00:25:12.090 --> 00:25:15.190
and you have your X, and
you can do the cooling.

00:25:15.190 --> 00:25:20.100
So this was Dave Pritchard's
suggestion in '89.

00:25:20.100 --> 00:25:23.360
So well, here are
two cooling methods

00:25:23.360 --> 00:25:25.970
developed by the people
who had their office

00:25:25.970 --> 00:25:29.000
next to me, laser cooling
and evaporative cooling.

00:25:29.000 --> 00:25:31.130
Laser cooling works
great initially,

00:25:31.130 --> 00:25:32.540
has certain limitations.

00:25:32.540 --> 00:25:35.720
But evaporative cooling
does not suffer from them.

00:25:35.720 --> 00:25:38.870
So it probably doesn't
require a rocket scientist

00:25:38.870 --> 00:25:41.180
to realize it would be
a good idea to combine

00:25:41.180 --> 00:25:43.100
the best of two worlds.

00:25:43.100 --> 00:25:45.860
So this was sort of the
idea in the early '90s.

00:25:45.860 --> 00:25:48.470
But there was a problem,
which some people regarded

00:25:48.470 --> 00:25:50.060
as fundamental.

00:25:50.060 --> 00:25:52.220
And the problem is
that, I mentioned

00:25:52.220 --> 00:25:56.210
that laser calling doesn't work
when the sample becomes opaque,

00:25:56.210 --> 00:25:59.690
when the light gets absorbed.

00:25:59.690 --> 00:26:01.820
On the other hand,
evaporative cooling

00:26:01.820 --> 00:26:04.310
requires collisions
between particles.

00:26:04.310 --> 00:26:07.730
One particle gets colder,
and one particle gets hotter.

00:26:07.730 --> 00:26:10.910
And now, if you look at the
collision cross-section,

00:26:10.910 --> 00:26:12.770
you face a dilemma.

00:26:12.770 --> 00:26:14.450
We don't want light
to be absorbed,

00:26:14.450 --> 00:26:16.490
but we want
collisions to happen.

00:26:16.490 --> 00:26:19.700
And the difference, the
ratio of those collision

00:26:19.700 --> 00:26:21.740
cross-sections effect of 1,000.

00:26:21.740 --> 00:26:24.110
So it seemed absolutely
impossible to have

00:26:24.110 --> 00:26:27.740
many collisions for evaporative
cooling, and at the same time

00:26:27.740 --> 00:26:32.360
avoid the complete
absorption of laser light.

00:26:32.360 --> 00:26:37.260
A solution, which Dave Pritchard
developed together with me was,

00:26:37.260 --> 00:26:39.270
so well, we can modify.

00:26:39.270 --> 00:26:43.620
We can push optical
cooling to higher densities

00:26:43.620 --> 00:26:45.840
by just playing a simple trick.

00:26:45.840 --> 00:26:51.100
If we hide the cold atoms,
in a different quantum state

00:26:51.100 --> 00:26:53.430
so that the light
is not absorbed,

00:26:53.430 --> 00:26:56.370
we can push laser cooling
to higher densities.

00:26:56.370 --> 00:26:59.490
I don't want to go into
details, but by modifying

00:26:59.490 --> 00:27:02.670
the standard knot,
which Dave co-invented,

00:27:02.670 --> 00:27:05.670
we realized something which
is called the dark spot

00:27:05.670 --> 00:27:08.850
And it was a key technique
to achieve Bose condensation

00:27:08.850 --> 00:27:11.490
both at MIT, and at Boulder.

00:27:11.490 --> 00:27:14.280
And I just showed you
the result, which at that

00:27:14.280 --> 00:27:18.880
point in '92, published in
'93 was fairly impressive.

00:27:18.880 --> 00:27:21.540
We were shining light
at a cloud of atoms,

00:27:21.540 --> 00:27:23.730
and the shadow was really black.

00:27:23.730 --> 00:27:26.460
We could infer that
the transmitted light

00:27:26.460 --> 00:27:28.740
was 10 to the minus-80.

00:27:28.740 --> 00:27:31.320
This was absolutely black.

00:27:31.320 --> 00:27:35.100
And so in '92 what
we had in our hands

00:27:35.100 --> 00:27:38.700
was an unprecedented
combination of high density

00:27:38.700 --> 00:27:40.890
and large number of atoms.

00:27:40.890 --> 00:27:43.360
And then, you told
stories about me, Dave.

00:27:43.360 --> 00:27:45.450
Now I have to tell
a story about you.

00:27:45.450 --> 00:27:49.680
Namely I still, I mean I
remember those dramatic days.

00:27:49.680 --> 00:27:52.620
This was the summer of
'92, and Dave Pritchard,

00:27:52.620 --> 00:27:55.080
he had funded work,
funded proposals,

00:27:55.080 --> 00:27:59.540
and he had great ideas to
study cold collisions, to study

00:27:59.540 --> 00:28:03.420
cold molecule formation,
and eventually many people

00:28:03.420 --> 00:28:05.710
got a lot of credit for that.

00:28:05.710 --> 00:28:07.971
But we had, so this
was the agenda,

00:28:07.971 --> 00:28:10.470
and this was the proposed work,
and this is what the funding

00:28:10.470 --> 00:28:12.300
agencies had supported.

00:28:12.300 --> 00:28:15.600
But then we sat together,
and decided maybe

00:28:15.600 --> 00:28:18.240
we can do something else with
those high density sample.

00:28:18.240 --> 00:28:22.500
Maybe we can go for the very
speculative, and challenging

00:28:22.500 --> 00:28:24.990
goal of Bose-Einstein
condensation.

00:28:24.990 --> 00:28:29.880
And it was for me
just this signature

00:28:29.880 --> 00:28:32.160
of a great scientist
that Dave said, okay,

00:28:32.160 --> 00:28:34.690
I give up all my ideas,
which were really great.

00:28:34.690 --> 00:28:38.270
And which other people got
enormous credit for that.

00:28:38.270 --> 00:28:39.840
Let's do what you suggest.

00:28:39.840 --> 00:28:41.550
Let's go for the long haul.

00:28:41.550 --> 00:28:43.650
Let's try to go
further, and use this

00:28:43.650 --> 00:28:45.540
as a starting
point for the quest

00:28:45.540 --> 00:28:47.740
for Bose-Einstein condensation.

00:28:47.740 --> 00:28:49.020
And it was very dramatic.

00:28:49.020 --> 00:28:51.900
Within a few weeks, even
before publishing the paper,

00:28:51.900 --> 00:28:54.720
we placed all the orders
for additional equipment

00:28:54.720 --> 00:28:56.370
necessary to do the next step.

00:28:56.370 --> 00:29:00.200
To combine laser cooling
with evaporative cooling.

00:29:00.200 --> 00:29:01.790
So eventually we
wanted to embark

00:29:01.790 --> 00:29:05.430
on the agenda to use laser
cooling as the first step.

00:29:05.430 --> 00:29:08.360
And then lower the temperature
by evaporative cooling,

00:29:08.360 --> 00:29:10.443
and get to Bose-Einstein
condensation.

00:29:13.690 --> 00:29:17.050
So well, we had to build a
complete machinery for that.

00:29:17.050 --> 00:29:19.940
And it took one or two
years to assemble it.

00:29:19.940 --> 00:29:21.520
And then we were sort of ready.

00:29:21.520 --> 00:29:24.070
And tried to see
evaporative cooling.

00:29:24.070 --> 00:29:30.510
And this, actually May of
'94 was an important step.

00:29:30.510 --> 00:29:33.510
I went to a meeting, and
announced for the first time

00:29:33.510 --> 00:29:35.640
that laser cooling,
and evaporative cooling

00:29:35.640 --> 00:29:36.900
had been combined.

00:29:36.900 --> 00:29:39.450
By using this high
density sample of atoms,

00:29:39.450 --> 00:29:43.250
we could bridge the gap between
this factor of thousands

00:29:43.250 --> 00:29:45.570
difference in cross section
of light absorption,

00:29:45.570 --> 00:29:47.010
elastic collision.

00:29:47.010 --> 00:29:49.125
We had seen evaporative
cooling, and now we

00:29:49.125 --> 00:29:51.570
were hoping to push further
to lower temperature.

00:29:51.570 --> 00:29:56.340
But still, we were five orders
of magnitude away from BEC.

00:29:56.340 --> 00:29:59.200
At the same meeting,
the Boulder group

00:29:59.200 --> 00:30:02.570
announced that they
accomplish something similar.

00:30:02.570 --> 00:30:05.860
So the race went on, and it was
one of the most exciting races

00:30:05.860 --> 00:30:06.670
of my lifetime.

00:30:06.670 --> 00:30:09.910
And Eric Cornell, Carl
Wieman, and myself, we

00:30:09.910 --> 00:30:10.780
remained friends.

00:30:10.780 --> 00:30:13.780
And we talked to each other
on the phone on Tuesday.

00:30:13.780 --> 00:30:16.510
And people would
say to each other,

00:30:16.510 --> 00:30:20.060
you got the best out of me
because if you have competition

00:30:20.060 --> 00:30:24.560
you work harder, you think
harder, and you do better work.

00:30:24.560 --> 00:30:27.120
Actually, at that
point I thought

00:30:27.120 --> 00:30:29.120
it could take the
rest of my life

00:30:29.120 --> 00:30:32.040
to push further
incrementally, and explore

00:30:32.040 --> 00:30:34.950
the new physics, which would
come about at higher densities,

00:30:34.950 --> 00:30:36.880
and lower temperatures.

00:30:36.880 --> 00:30:40.110
And I knew that the quest for
Bose-Einstein condensation

00:30:40.110 --> 00:30:42.940
in Tom Greytaks, and
Dan Kleppner's lab

00:30:42.940 --> 00:30:45.010
had already lasted 15 years.

00:30:45.010 --> 00:30:48.070
And I was expecting,
so well we trying

00:30:48.070 --> 00:30:49.130
on something alternative.

00:30:49.130 --> 00:30:51.460
And let's see which
method works better.

00:30:51.460 --> 00:30:53.260
And to the big, big
surprise, and it

00:30:53.260 --> 00:30:55.750
came completely
unexpected, just one year

00:30:55.750 --> 00:30:58.660
later Bose-Einstein
condensation was achieved.

00:30:58.660 --> 00:31:00.790
It's one of the rare
examples, I think,

00:31:00.790 --> 00:31:03.250
where you think you do
an incremental step,

00:31:03.250 --> 00:31:07.090
and you step forward by
a factor of a million.

00:31:07.090 --> 00:31:09.880
So these were very
dramatic moments.

00:31:09.880 --> 00:31:13.060
Let me mention a little bit
about the last obstacles

00:31:13.060 --> 00:31:15.310
on the finish line to
Bose-Einstein condensation.

00:31:15.310 --> 00:31:17.890
So the combination of
evaporative cooling, and laser

00:31:17.890 --> 00:31:19.750
cooling was accomplished.

00:31:19.750 --> 00:31:25.060
But in order to have enough
collisions between the atoms

00:31:25.060 --> 00:31:27.280
to have good evaporation,
we had to keep

00:31:27.280 --> 00:31:29.470
the gas tightly confined.

00:31:29.470 --> 00:31:35.200
So we needed tightly confining
containers, or magnetic traps.

00:31:35.200 --> 00:31:37.000
Now this may be a
little bit technical,

00:31:37.000 --> 00:31:40.190
but some people in the
audience who probably

00:31:40.190 --> 00:31:42.400
are interested in that.

00:31:42.400 --> 00:31:46.540
There are two types
of magnetic traps.

00:31:46.540 --> 00:31:51.040
Some have a pointy potentially,
a linear re-shape potential.

00:31:51.040 --> 00:31:54.310
And others have a round
potential at the bottom.

00:31:54.310 --> 00:31:56.800
Those traps are more
tightly confining,

00:31:56.800 --> 00:31:59.950
and that's what both the
group in Boulder and at MIT

00:31:59.950 --> 00:32:03.430
used to demonstrate initial
evaporative cooling because it

00:32:03.430 --> 00:32:05.455
makes it easier to
get many collisions,

00:32:05.455 --> 00:32:07.370
and see evaporation.

00:32:07.370 --> 00:32:09.200
But they have a problem.

00:32:09.200 --> 00:32:12.380
There is a point of zero
magnetic field in the center.

00:32:12.380 --> 00:32:14.400
And if the field
is zero, the atoms

00:32:14.400 --> 00:32:16.390
don't know where to
spin up, and spin down.

00:32:16.390 --> 00:32:20.980
They lose their orientation, and
virtually flip out of the trap.

00:32:20.980 --> 00:32:22.480
So we lost the atoms.

00:32:22.480 --> 00:32:25.690
It's like a hole
where atoms leak out.

00:32:25.690 --> 00:32:28.370
And the months before
BEC was achieved,

00:32:28.370 --> 00:32:31.020
both groups had
to invent methods.

00:32:31.020 --> 00:32:34.750
And this, how it was dubbed,
how to plug the hole.

00:32:34.750 --> 00:32:38.190
Eric Cornell's group in Boulder
used an ingenious method

00:32:38.190 --> 00:32:40.440
with rotating magnetic fields.

00:32:40.440 --> 00:32:43.080
And we decided to use
a different method.

00:32:43.080 --> 00:32:45.810
We wanted to plug the hole
with a laser beam, which

00:32:45.810 --> 00:32:49.490
was keeping the atoms away
from this dangerous region

00:32:49.490 --> 00:32:52.620
where they would just fall
out of the magnetic trap.

00:32:52.620 --> 00:32:54.960
So what we created
was a potential,

00:32:54.960 --> 00:32:56.570
which has a hole in the middle.

00:32:56.570 --> 00:32:59.537
And you see here the
shape of our clouds.

00:32:59.537 --> 00:33:00.370
And it worked great.

00:33:00.370 --> 00:33:01.520
It was really dramatic.

00:33:01.520 --> 00:33:04.430
We could push forward
orders of magnitude

00:33:04.430 --> 00:33:07.530
towards Bose-Einstein
condensation.

00:33:07.530 --> 00:33:10.650
And then, this was
in June of '95,

00:33:10.650 --> 00:33:12.810
we heard about the
Boulder results.

00:33:12.810 --> 00:33:17.094
They had succeeded to get
Bose-Einstein condensation.

00:33:17.094 --> 00:33:18.760
And that's maybe
something, I don't know

00:33:18.760 --> 00:33:20.830
if I've shared that publicly.

00:33:20.830 --> 00:33:23.680
If you're an
assistant professor,

00:33:23.680 --> 00:33:26.260
you put all your money,
all your resources,

00:33:26.260 --> 00:33:32.730
all your efforts in one
goal, and then you're

00:33:32.730 --> 00:33:36.280
notified that somebody
else has accomplished it.

00:33:36.280 --> 00:33:37.540
This is a difficult situation.

00:33:37.540 --> 00:33:40.310
[AUDIENCE LAUGHS]

00:33:40.310 --> 00:33:41.990
You wonder about promotion.

00:33:41.990 --> 00:33:44.430
You wonder about your
scientific career,

00:33:44.430 --> 00:33:47.150
and of course I know at
MIT the bar is very high.

00:33:47.150 --> 00:33:50.060
MIT is not keeping losers.

00:33:50.060 --> 00:33:52.040
So I was really
scratching my head.

00:33:52.040 --> 00:33:53.480
I woke up early in the morning.

00:33:53.480 --> 00:33:54.740
What to do?

00:33:54.740 --> 00:33:56.750
And people ask me, why
don't you just copy

00:33:56.750 --> 00:33:57.890
the design in Boulder?

00:33:57.890 --> 00:34:01.190
And I mean this seems to be
a successful way to do it.

00:34:01.190 --> 00:34:03.170
And then a whole
new field opens up.

00:34:03.170 --> 00:34:06.085
And we had fierce
discussion in my group.

00:34:06.085 --> 00:34:09.790
And what we did is actually
a two-fold strategy.

00:34:09.790 --> 00:34:11.780
We designed a new magnetic trap.

00:34:11.780 --> 00:34:12.820
I will talk about it.

00:34:12.820 --> 00:34:15.159
And this turned into the
workhorse of the field.

00:34:15.159 --> 00:34:17.380
And this has given us
an enormous productivity

00:34:17.380 --> 00:34:19.159
over the last few years.

00:34:19.159 --> 00:34:22.120
But, and I was sort of
willing to say let's

00:34:22.120 --> 00:34:26.130
just put all our eggs
into this basket.

00:34:26.130 --> 00:34:28.510
Let's pursue that, because
this new magnetic trap is

00:34:28.510 --> 00:34:29.750
a really good idea.

00:34:29.750 --> 00:34:32.340
And indeed, it turned
out to be a good idea.

00:34:32.340 --> 00:34:35.760
But I work with a good group
of people, and they told me,

00:34:35.760 --> 00:34:39.239
so well, this trap with the
plug, maybe it will work.

00:34:39.239 --> 00:34:41.520
Or even if it doesn't work,
because maybe the laser

00:34:41.520 --> 00:34:43.760
beam is jittering, and
heating up the atoms.

00:34:43.760 --> 00:34:45.510
Let's just give it
one, or two more tries.

00:34:45.510 --> 00:34:47.489
And that's really what,
let's just figure out

00:34:47.489 --> 00:34:49.370
how far we've come,
because the Boulder

00:34:49.370 --> 00:34:51.120
group has a big success.

00:34:51.120 --> 00:34:55.960
And we should at least
document how close we came.

00:34:55.960 --> 00:34:59.000
So well, I try to listen to
my collaborators, and said,

00:34:59.000 --> 00:35:00.290
okay, but just a few times.

00:35:00.290 --> 00:35:01.490
We can't waste time.

00:35:01.490 --> 00:35:03.170
We don't want to miss the boat.

00:35:03.170 --> 00:35:06.050
And it was one of the
very last attempts

00:35:06.050 --> 00:35:09.350
to see how far we could
push with our original idea

00:35:09.350 --> 00:35:11.600
of this optically
plugged trap that we

00:35:11.600 --> 00:35:13.839
saw Bose-Einstein condensation.

00:35:13.839 --> 00:35:15.630
This is a picture,
which doesn't look nice,

00:35:15.630 --> 00:35:18.990
but I want to show it
to sort of the experts.

00:35:18.990 --> 00:35:21.890
This was our first signal of
Bose-Einstein condensation

00:35:21.890 --> 00:35:24.700
on September 30th in '95.

00:35:24.700 --> 00:35:26.230
We were not really
ready to observe.

00:35:26.230 --> 00:35:29.330
We just wanted to see where are
the limits of the plugged trap.

00:35:29.330 --> 00:35:33.290
And for the experts, we couldn't
switch off the laser beam.

00:35:33.290 --> 00:35:36.070
So in time of flight,
the atoms were just

00:35:36.070 --> 00:35:37.687
pushed away by the laser beam.

00:35:37.687 --> 00:35:39.520
So it's not a pretty
time of flight picture.

00:35:39.520 --> 00:35:41.680
It doesn't look as
pretty as the other ones.

00:35:41.680 --> 00:35:44.200
But we saw something black.

00:35:44.200 --> 00:35:46.880
Sharp, black spots
on the screen.

00:35:46.880 --> 00:35:49.540
And if a cloud
expands ballistically.

00:35:49.540 --> 00:35:52.810
And stays compact, that means
there's very little velocity.

00:35:52.810 --> 00:35:54.160
There is very little motion.

00:35:54.160 --> 00:35:55.900
It's very, very cold.

00:35:55.900 --> 00:35:58.810
And we got enormously excited.

00:35:58.810 --> 00:36:00.380
And within a few
hours, we worked

00:36:00.380 --> 00:36:01.940
throughout the whole night.

00:36:01.940 --> 00:36:04.780
We improved the set up,
and we got clear evidence

00:36:04.780 --> 00:36:07.602
that we had observed
Bose-Einstein condensation.

00:36:10.320 --> 00:36:12.670
We were running the
experiment only one more

00:36:12.670 --> 00:36:15.160
time to get final data.

00:36:15.160 --> 00:36:17.890
And this is actually,
for me, still amazing.

00:36:17.890 --> 00:36:20.035
This is the quality of the data.

00:36:20.035 --> 00:36:22.510
Excuse me.

00:36:22.510 --> 00:36:24.550
Which was obtained
the second time

00:36:24.550 --> 00:36:28.320
we were ever running the machine
for Bose-Einstein condensation.

00:36:28.320 --> 00:36:30.020
Let me just explain
what it means.

00:36:30.020 --> 00:36:32.980
We let the gas go,
and it expands out.

00:36:32.980 --> 00:36:35.320
And then we take a
snapshot, and the larger

00:36:35.320 --> 00:36:37.840
the cloud is, the
faster is the velocity,

00:36:37.840 --> 00:36:40.330
the hotter are the atoms.

00:36:40.330 --> 00:36:42.550
And now you see what
happens when we cool down.

00:36:42.550 --> 00:36:45.120
You see how this cloud
is sort of shrinking

00:36:45.120 --> 00:36:46.830
because the atom slowed down.

00:36:46.830 --> 00:36:48.960
And this is sort of
the last factor of two.

00:36:48.960 --> 00:36:51.210
The last fraction of
a second to achieve

00:36:51.210 --> 00:36:54.530
Bose-Einstein condensation, and
then this new form of matter.

00:36:54.530 --> 00:36:58.380
This ultra cold gas, which is a
Bose-Einstein condensate forms.

00:36:58.380 --> 00:36:59.220
It's very visual.

00:36:59.220 --> 00:37:01.230
It's very dramatic.

00:37:01.230 --> 00:37:03.860
And we obtained that without
really optimizing our machine.

00:37:08.297 --> 00:37:13.470
Okay, so I mentioned that this
was a night on September 30th.

00:37:13.470 --> 00:37:17.450
And so well, at MIT
you have to teach.

00:37:17.450 --> 00:37:20.430
And October 1st I
had to teach 8012.

00:37:20.430 --> 00:37:23.070
[AUDIENCE LAUGHS]

00:37:23.070 --> 00:37:26.970
I received an email message
from one of my students now,

00:37:26.970 --> 00:37:28.500
six years later.

00:37:28.500 --> 00:37:33.000
As a freshman in the fall of
'95, I took your 8012 class.

00:37:33.000 --> 00:37:35.910
I remember one day when
you were unusually late

00:37:35.910 --> 00:37:38.070
for your lecture,
and you came rushing

00:37:38.070 --> 00:37:41.880
in very excited, yet very
tired from the previous night's

00:37:41.880 --> 00:37:43.560
experiment.

00:37:43.560 --> 00:37:46.890
I think I told the students I
didn't get any sleep at all.

00:37:46.890 --> 00:37:50.250
And please, if I fall
asleep during my class,

00:37:50.250 --> 00:37:51.694
ask me a question.

00:37:51.694 --> 00:37:55.152
[AUDIENCE LAUGHS]

00:37:56.140 --> 00:37:58.780
So well, a few weeks later
there was some short report

00:37:58.780 --> 00:38:00.850
in Tick Tock, but
this was the night

00:38:00.850 --> 00:38:05.930
that Bose-Einstein condensation
was realized at MIT.

00:38:05.930 --> 00:38:09.390
Okay, but immediately
with our approach,

00:38:09.390 --> 00:38:11.310
we had 100 times more atoms.

00:38:11.310 --> 00:38:13.860
And we had a system,
which was more

00:38:13.860 --> 00:38:16.200
versatile to pursue
the new physics

00:38:16.200 --> 00:38:18.810
of Bose-Einstein condensation.

00:38:18.810 --> 00:38:21.510
This is a picture of the
group a few months earlier.

00:38:21.510 --> 00:38:23.280
It doesn't show
Ken Davis, but it

00:38:23.280 --> 00:38:25.910
shows the people, the other
people who were on board.

00:38:25.910 --> 00:38:29.000
Dylan Durfee, Mark
Mavis, Michael Andrews,

00:38:29.000 --> 00:38:32.040
Ben Stamper-Kurn, Klaas-Jan
van Druten as a post-doc,

00:38:32.040 --> 00:38:36.080
and Chris Townsend joined a
few months after we saw BEC.

00:38:36.080 --> 00:38:38.430
Anyway, we were
playing, and I'll

00:38:38.430 --> 00:38:42.480
tell you a story of sometimes
you do the right thing

00:38:42.480 --> 00:38:44.580
for the wrong reason.

00:38:44.580 --> 00:38:49.090
And this is when we changed our
magnetic trap, which is really

00:38:49.090 --> 00:38:50.770
the core of the apparatus.

00:38:50.770 --> 00:38:54.220
We were one of the
two or three groups

00:38:54.220 --> 00:38:56.060
that was a little bit
ubiquitous observation

00:38:56.060 --> 00:38:59.120
by a third group, who had seen
Bose-Einstein condensation.

00:38:59.120 --> 00:39:01.880
And everybody was
brimming with excitement,

00:39:01.880 --> 00:39:03.260
and I made a wrong assessment.

00:39:03.260 --> 00:39:06.770
I said, hey it wasn't really
so complicated, what we do.

00:39:06.770 --> 00:39:09.710
In the next few months,
many groups around the world

00:39:09.710 --> 00:39:11.049
will repeat it.

00:39:11.049 --> 00:39:12.590
And now we have to
be really careful.

00:39:12.590 --> 00:39:15.740
Maybe with our jittery laser
beam, we're not competitive.

00:39:15.740 --> 00:39:18.810
We really have to go forward,
and make an improvement.

00:39:18.810 --> 00:39:22.190
And we thought, let's
build a new magnetic trap.

00:39:22.190 --> 00:39:24.590
What happened, actually,
is it took us five months

00:39:24.590 --> 00:39:25.340
to build it.

00:39:25.340 --> 00:39:26.960
We couldn't get any
Bose condensation

00:39:26.960 --> 00:39:29.380
for the next five months.

00:39:29.380 --> 00:39:32.610
But when we got it, we were
really going like gangbusters.

00:39:32.610 --> 00:39:35.010
But my assessment was
wrong, because nobody else

00:39:35.010 --> 00:39:37.500
got Bose condensation
in the meantime.

00:39:37.500 --> 00:39:40.620
Indeed, to build such
a complex apparatus it

00:39:40.620 --> 00:39:43.260
took two years for
the other groups.

00:39:43.260 --> 00:39:45.810
So if we had known that, we
would have probably just stuck

00:39:45.810 --> 00:39:47.400
to our original set up.

00:39:47.400 --> 00:39:49.590
But we went forward,
and created what

00:39:49.590 --> 00:39:52.410
turned out to be a
good trap, and which

00:39:52.410 --> 00:39:54.280
gave us enormous productivity.

00:39:54.280 --> 00:39:56.670
So we realized this
trapping configuration,

00:39:56.670 --> 00:39:59.340
which is not suffering from
this leak, it's sort of a round

00:39:59.340 --> 00:40:00.750
bottom trap.

00:40:00.750 --> 00:40:02.880
It actually carries the
name Ioffe-Pritchard

00:40:02.880 --> 00:40:07.440
because Dave Pritchard
suggested that in the mid '80s.

00:40:07.440 --> 00:40:09.630
And we found a new
way to wind the trap.

00:40:09.630 --> 00:40:11.760
It's now referred to
as a cloverleaf trap.

00:40:11.760 --> 00:40:15.475
And so well, this is how we
have produced Bose condensates

00:40:15.475 --> 00:40:18.150
ever since.

00:40:18.150 --> 00:40:21.280
This shows you the
actual winding path.

00:40:21.280 --> 00:40:23.560
And now I show
you a few examples

00:40:23.560 --> 00:40:25.720
of what we were able
to do in this trap.

00:40:25.720 --> 00:40:28.090
It was an enormously
productive time,

00:40:28.090 --> 00:40:30.850
and one of my
graduate students, who

00:40:30.850 --> 00:40:37.560
was with me between '95 and
'99, he graduated with 20 papers

00:40:37.560 --> 00:40:41.581
in [INAUDIBLE],,
Nature, or Science.

00:40:41.581 --> 00:40:43.330
So these were one of
the first experiments

00:40:43.330 --> 00:40:45.130
we did in this novel trap.

00:40:45.130 --> 00:40:48.380
We could now visualize the
condensate inside the trap

00:40:48.380 --> 00:40:50.350
using a novel light
scattering technique,

00:40:50.350 --> 00:40:53.810
and now we could really
observe what was going on

00:40:53.810 --> 00:40:55.340
inside the condensate.

00:40:55.340 --> 00:40:57.020
And here you see the cool down.

00:40:57.020 --> 00:40:59.670
Our magnetic container
was elongated,

00:40:59.670 --> 00:41:03.030
so it's an elongated cloud,
and it shrinks as we cool.

00:41:03.030 --> 00:41:05.030
And when we reach
the phase transition

00:41:05.030 --> 00:41:08.030
in the middle of that
trap we see this formation

00:41:08.030 --> 00:41:11.270
of a dense core that
Bose-Einstein condensate.

00:41:11.270 --> 00:41:14.270
And if you just take a
cut through this cloud

00:41:14.270 --> 00:41:17.050
you can see, you know, this.

00:41:17.050 --> 00:41:19.520
You remember the blue guys
who march in lockstep?

00:41:19.520 --> 00:41:23.060
This is this peak, and then
you have a thermal component.

00:41:23.060 --> 00:41:25.790
These were the colorful guys
which were moving and whizzing

00:41:25.790 --> 00:41:28.250
around in all directions.

00:41:28.250 --> 00:41:30.110
And we did a series
of experiments.

00:41:30.110 --> 00:41:33.560
Here you see a condensate
set in oscillation.

00:41:33.560 --> 00:41:35.360
People call it the
study of sound,

00:41:35.360 --> 00:41:37.970
and some of those
results were just

00:41:37.970 --> 00:41:39.410
the frequency of oscillation.

00:41:39.410 --> 00:41:42.990
What is its damping was really
causing major challenges

00:41:42.990 --> 00:41:48.250
to many body theory, and led to
hundreds of theoretical papers.

00:41:48.250 --> 00:41:51.430
On something which I
felt was very dramatic,

00:41:51.430 --> 00:41:54.220
those nano-Kelvin atoms, you
think they are so fragile.

00:41:54.220 --> 00:41:56.920
It's colder than anything
else, but by using

00:41:56.920 --> 00:41:58.790
this light scattering
technique and just

00:41:58.790 --> 00:42:02.560
stroboscopic illumination
we could really see how,

00:42:02.560 --> 00:42:05.760
color-coded in red,
how condensate formed.

00:42:05.760 --> 00:42:10.660
It was for me like watching
how nature is giving birth

00:42:10.660 --> 00:42:12.940
to something, which
is very fragile,

00:42:12.940 --> 00:42:15.670
but nevertheless we
were able to observe it

00:42:15.670 --> 00:42:18.130
in its natural environment.

00:42:18.130 --> 00:42:20.890
And actually, the way how
the condensate forms was

00:42:20.890 --> 00:42:23.620
challenging theory, and
over many, many years

00:42:23.620 --> 00:42:27.850
now, quantum kinetics theory has
been developed, to some extent,

00:42:27.850 --> 00:42:32.230
to explain the dynamics of the
formation of the condensate.

00:42:32.230 --> 00:42:45.740
But let me now talk about two
examples of our research, which

00:42:45.740 --> 00:42:51.210
really demonstrates what makes
Bose condensate so special.

00:42:51.210 --> 00:42:54.550
Now, if I talk to
a lay audience,

00:42:54.550 --> 00:42:57.920
so if I want to help you.

00:42:57.920 --> 00:43:00.530
If you meet your friends,
and you want to tell them,

00:43:00.530 --> 00:43:04.390
what is really special about
Bose-Einstein condensates?

00:43:04.390 --> 00:43:07.470
I want to now give
you two examples how

00:43:07.470 --> 00:43:10.510
you can convince people
that this is something

00:43:10.510 --> 00:43:12.420
that has unusual properties.

00:43:12.420 --> 00:43:15.720
And it's really related
to what I call it here,

00:43:15.720 --> 00:43:17.410
the magic of matter waves.

00:43:17.410 --> 00:43:19.590
Now I want to show
you two examples.

00:43:19.590 --> 00:43:23.070
The interference of
condensates, which

00:43:23.070 --> 00:43:26.840
allow you to directly
photograph matter waves.

00:43:26.840 --> 00:43:28.730
And I want to show
you the example

00:43:28.730 --> 00:43:31.640
of rotating
condensates, which have

00:43:31.640 --> 00:43:33.710
a Swiss cheese-like appearance.

00:43:33.710 --> 00:43:40.246
This is very different from how
any ordinary matter behaves.

00:43:40.246 --> 00:43:43.030
Okay.

00:43:43.030 --> 00:43:45.370
What I want to
explain to you now,

00:43:45.370 --> 00:43:47.210
the interference
between condensates

00:43:47.210 --> 00:43:49.760
was actually a key
experiment which we did.

00:43:49.760 --> 00:43:55.790
And it was also actually one
of our key contributions,

00:43:55.790 --> 00:44:00.620
which was emphasized
by the Swedish Academy.

00:44:00.620 --> 00:44:05.770
Because what we could do in
'96, and publish in '97 was we

00:44:05.770 --> 00:44:08.830
could show for the first time
that the condensate is not

00:44:08.830 --> 00:44:10.930
really cold.

00:44:10.930 --> 00:44:12.880
So well, coldness is
relative, but that it

00:44:12.880 --> 00:44:15.820
has these special properties
that all the atoms march

00:44:15.820 --> 00:44:16.540
in lockstep.

00:44:16.540 --> 00:44:20.280
That they're just one quantum
mechanical matter wave.

00:44:20.280 --> 00:44:22.777
Okay, if you want to
show that something,

00:44:22.777 --> 00:44:24.860
if you want to show, for
instance, that this laser

00:44:24.860 --> 00:44:29.180
beam is an electromagnetic wave,
the best way to do it is you

00:44:29.180 --> 00:44:32.000
take two laser beams,
and interfere them.

00:44:32.000 --> 00:44:34.160
And then you see an
interference pattern.

00:44:34.160 --> 00:44:38.970
The interference is the clear
evidence for the wave nature.

00:44:38.970 --> 00:44:42.860
So we needed to condensates, and
so well by using a laser knife,

00:44:42.860 --> 00:44:45.080
and cutting a condensate
into two pieces,

00:44:45.080 --> 00:44:46.820
foregoing all the details.

00:44:46.820 --> 00:44:48.860
We created two condensates.

00:44:48.860 --> 00:44:51.650
And so now we had
two condensates,

00:44:51.650 --> 00:44:55.505
and we switched off our magnetic
container, and the condensates,

00:44:55.505 --> 00:44:57.590
so well, if you switch
it off, the condensates

00:44:57.590 --> 00:44:59.420
fall down due to gravity.

00:44:59.420 --> 00:45:02.600
But they also expand because
of zero point motion,

00:45:02.600 --> 00:45:04.910
and atomic repulsion.

00:45:04.910 --> 00:45:07.550
So what we are hoping
for is as the condensates

00:45:07.550 --> 00:45:11.030
expand into each other,
and overlap to see

00:45:11.030 --> 00:45:14.510
the wave nature of those atoms.

00:45:14.510 --> 00:45:19.540
Certainly showed you
what we expected to see.

00:45:19.540 --> 00:45:22.020
So let me just give you an
example for the interference

00:45:22.020 --> 00:45:23.530
between two sources.

00:45:23.530 --> 00:45:27.960
So assume that this is the
antenna of our favorite radio

00:45:27.960 --> 00:45:31.630
station in Boston, and
it radiates radio waves.

00:45:31.630 --> 00:45:35.740
And now we bring in a
second radio station,

00:45:35.740 --> 00:45:39.190
and now the waves of the two
radio stations interfere.

00:45:39.190 --> 00:45:41.530
And now you see the
interference pattern.

00:45:41.530 --> 00:45:43.900
And of course, the
interference pattern

00:45:43.900 --> 00:45:49.910
becomes more microscopic
the closer the distance is.

00:45:49.910 --> 00:45:52.220
And at least those
who took 803, they

00:45:52.220 --> 00:45:55.870
should realize that this
interference pattern has

00:45:55.870 --> 00:45:59.060
the form of parabolas,
because a parabola is

00:45:59.060 --> 00:46:02.500
a mathematical curve for which
the path length difference

00:46:02.500 --> 00:46:06.380
to the two origins is constant.

00:46:06.380 --> 00:46:09.260
So well, we're not
interfering radio waves,

00:46:09.260 --> 00:46:11.180
we're interfering matter waves.

00:46:11.180 --> 00:46:14.710
So we have our container,
the atoms spread out,

00:46:14.710 --> 00:46:16.880
and I have to
remind you probably

00:46:16.880 --> 00:46:20.090
of pretty much the simple
equation I showed you,

00:46:20.090 --> 00:46:22.010
the de Broglie relation.

00:46:22.010 --> 00:46:24.350
The faster the atoms
are, the shorter

00:46:24.350 --> 00:46:26.330
is the de Broglie wavelengths.

00:46:26.330 --> 00:46:28.730
So if you have a puff
of gas, and let it go,

00:46:28.730 --> 00:46:30.800
and then take a
snapshot, the atoms

00:46:30.800 --> 00:46:33.890
which are further out they
have moved there faster.

00:46:33.890 --> 00:46:37.340
And the atoms which are
closer in, they are slower.

00:46:37.340 --> 00:46:40.750
So the wave pattern
of such a pulsed

00:46:40.750 --> 00:46:43.060
matter wave looks like this.

00:46:43.060 --> 00:46:45.790
The matter wavelengths
is longer where the atoms

00:46:45.790 --> 00:46:48.740
haven't traveled so far, and
it is shorter where they have

00:46:48.740 --> 00:46:50.450
travelled further.

00:46:50.450 --> 00:46:55.880
So now we interfere our two
Bose-Einstein condensates.

00:46:55.880 --> 00:46:58.930
And you again see now, as we
bring them closer together,

00:46:58.930 --> 00:47:01.940
that there is a distinct
interference pattern.

00:47:01.940 --> 00:47:04.640
But in contrast to
the two radio station,

00:47:04.640 --> 00:47:07.830
this interference
pattern consists

00:47:07.830 --> 00:47:10.110
of perfectly straight lines.

00:47:17.050 --> 00:47:19.750
And when we observe the
overlap of the two condensates,

00:47:19.750 --> 00:47:24.630
and we saw that, we were really
jumping up from our chairs.

00:47:24.630 --> 00:47:27.495
And I think I remember the
first night we saw that.

00:47:27.495 --> 00:47:30.490
Again, we had worked into
the wee hours in the morning.

00:47:30.490 --> 00:47:33.240
I think it was you, Dan,
you came in at 8:00 or 9:00,

00:47:33.240 --> 00:47:37.010
at 8 or 9 o'clock and
I was almost drunk,

00:47:37.010 --> 00:47:38.194
and I came to Dan's office.

00:47:38.194 --> 00:47:39.610
Dan, I have to
show you something.

00:47:39.610 --> 00:47:43.060
And Dan came to the office,
and Dan came to the lab

00:47:43.060 --> 00:47:45.070
and we had this
pattern on the screen.

00:47:45.070 --> 00:47:49.150
So we knew the Bose condensate
is a coherent form of matter.

00:47:49.150 --> 00:47:53.020
Actually, just to talk in terms
of layperson, what you actually

00:47:53.020 --> 00:47:55.352
see here is the--

00:47:55.352 --> 00:47:56.060
well, two things.

00:47:56.060 --> 00:47:57.940
One is, this is a
direct observation

00:47:57.940 --> 00:47:59.810
of the wave nature of matter.

00:47:59.810 --> 00:48:04.250
We simply take two puffs
of atoms, they overlap,

00:48:04.250 --> 00:48:06.760
and then we illuminate the
atoms with a laser beam.

00:48:06.760 --> 00:48:08.460
And we take a shadow image.

00:48:08.460 --> 00:48:11.230
There is, it's the
most direct observation

00:48:11.230 --> 00:48:13.510
of the nature of matter.

00:48:13.510 --> 00:48:17.330
But secondly, this
is also demonstrating

00:48:17.330 --> 00:48:22.530
in a fairly dramatic way, this
equation that atoms plus atoms,

00:48:22.530 --> 00:48:23.900
gives vacuum.

00:48:23.900 --> 00:48:26.790
Because where there is no
shadow, there are no atoms.

00:48:26.790 --> 00:48:29.770
So the two matter waves
interfere each other away.

00:48:29.770 --> 00:48:32.866
Atoms plus atoms gives vacuum.

00:48:32.866 --> 00:48:36.570
I mean, I sometimes get
fan mail from people

00:48:36.570 --> 00:48:38.690
with their own theories
of physics, and.

00:48:38.690 --> 00:48:42.110
[AUDIENCE LAUGHS]

00:48:42.110 --> 00:48:43.850
And I got some
questions about, yeah,

00:48:43.850 --> 00:48:46.730
what happens if you annihilate
atoms by interference,

00:48:46.730 --> 00:48:47.930
but I think you all know.

00:48:47.930 --> 00:48:49.730
The atoms which
are missing here,

00:48:49.730 --> 00:48:53.660
they appear in the dark
fringes because atom plus atom

00:48:53.660 --> 00:48:55.340
equals four atoms.

00:48:55.340 --> 00:48:58.380
If you have coherent
interference.

00:48:58.380 --> 00:49:00.540
Okay, so this is just
a different, more fancy

00:49:00.540 --> 00:49:02.570
representation of that.

00:49:02.570 --> 00:49:08.220
At about the same time, we found
a trick to release atoms out

00:49:08.220 --> 00:49:09.380
of the magnetic trap.

00:49:09.380 --> 00:49:11.670
We were just sort of
spin-flipping atoms.

00:49:11.670 --> 00:49:14.400
They were accelerated
down by gravity,

00:49:14.400 --> 00:49:18.060
and so within a few months
we had done two things.

00:49:18.060 --> 00:49:21.030
We had shown that the
atoms are laser-like,

00:49:21.030 --> 00:49:25.830
and secondly we were able to
create pulse beams of atoms.

00:49:25.830 --> 00:49:28.880
And this together
is now recognized

00:49:28.880 --> 00:49:31.860
as the first realization
of an atom laser.

00:49:31.860 --> 00:49:35.790
An atom laser, which
emits atomic matter

00:49:35.790 --> 00:49:37.860
with laser light properties.

00:49:37.860 --> 00:49:40.440
And other groups have
followed, and improved on that.

00:49:40.440 --> 00:49:42.780
So this is sort of an
atom laser gallery.

00:49:42.780 --> 00:49:45.660
Continues atom lasers
and in different ways

00:49:45.660 --> 00:49:50.056
its a rich field, and
there's a lot to be done.

00:49:50.056 --> 00:49:53.740
Let me now talk about
this other example,

00:49:53.740 --> 00:49:59.870
and where the wave nature of
matter really becomes manifest.

00:49:59.870 --> 00:50:02.810
And this is actually related
to a very special property

00:50:02.810 --> 00:50:04.400
of the Bose condensate.

00:50:04.400 --> 00:50:06.870
The Bose condensate is
sort of one giant matter

00:50:06.870 --> 00:50:10.250
wave, and its possible
for wave-like matter

00:50:10.250 --> 00:50:13.910
to move, to propagate
without any friction.

00:50:13.910 --> 00:50:16.190
This one similar
system which has

00:50:16.190 --> 00:50:20.120
enormous technical importance,
and these are super conductors.

00:50:20.120 --> 00:50:22.460
In superconductors
electricity can

00:50:22.460 --> 00:50:25.566
flow without any dissipation.

00:50:25.566 --> 00:50:26.940
You can switch on
a ring current,

00:50:26.940 --> 00:50:30.050
and the ring-- you can go
home, and months later the ring

00:50:30.050 --> 00:50:33.870
current still flows
without any active power.

00:50:33.870 --> 00:50:36.545
Those phenomena are
usually dubbed, "super."

00:50:36.545 --> 00:50:39.990
Super conductivity for
electrons, super fluidity

00:50:39.990 --> 00:50:41.900
for liquid helium.

00:50:41.900 --> 00:50:44.780
And here we have
a system which was

00:50:44.780 --> 00:50:47.900
predicted to be super
fluid, but its a gas.

00:50:47.900 --> 00:50:53.160
Its eight orders of magnitude
more dilute the liquid helium.

00:50:53.160 --> 00:50:55.040
So we have the most
fundamental system

00:50:55.040 --> 00:50:57.590
in our hands to
understand details

00:50:57.590 --> 00:51:01.370
of super fluidity or
macroscopic random phenomena.

00:51:01.370 --> 00:51:03.820
I don't want to go into
details, and rather

00:51:03.820 --> 00:51:07.980
emphasize one aspect,
which is fairly directly

00:51:07.980 --> 00:51:09.450
connected to superfluity.

00:51:09.450 --> 00:51:11.220
But I'm not really
explaining it,

00:51:11.220 --> 00:51:14.390
and this is the
physics of vortices.

00:51:14.390 --> 00:51:17.210
Now, I think you are
familiar with vortices.

00:51:17.210 --> 00:51:20.810
This as a vortex on
a very large scale.

00:51:20.810 --> 00:51:25.220
You see how they can,
off the coast of Florida.

00:51:25.220 --> 00:51:29.840
I think we also encounter
vortices in our everyday life.

00:51:29.840 --> 00:51:31.420
At least, I could
used one today.

00:51:31.420 --> 00:51:33.320
[AUDIENCE LAUGHS]

00:51:33.320 --> 00:51:37.160
You simply push the button,
and you create a vortex.

00:51:37.160 --> 00:51:41.360
So what is special
about vortex physics?

00:51:41.360 --> 00:51:43.700
Okay, there's something
important difference.

00:51:43.700 --> 00:51:45.800
And that's what I
want to explain.

00:51:45.800 --> 00:51:48.782
Let's consider this
rotating bucket experiment.

00:51:48.782 --> 00:51:50.240
And I want to show
you that there's

00:51:50.240 --> 00:51:55.020
a dramatic difference between
rotating, and ordinary fluid.

00:51:55.020 --> 00:51:57.020
Let's say a bucket
full of water,

00:51:57.020 --> 00:52:00.920
and rotating what I
call a quantum fluid.

00:52:00.920 --> 00:52:03.920
And this is the system where
all the particles are just

00:52:03.920 --> 00:52:05.780
one big wave.

00:52:05.780 --> 00:52:08.690
First of all, if you
rotate a normal fluid,

00:52:08.690 --> 00:52:12.560
you know that upon the rotation
it forms this parabolic surface

00:52:12.560 --> 00:52:15.460
because of centrifugal forces.

00:52:15.460 --> 00:52:17.680
If you take, for instance,
superfluid helium,

00:52:17.680 --> 00:52:19.660
and you do the same.

00:52:19.660 --> 00:52:21.610
I just told you there's
a big difference,

00:52:21.610 --> 00:52:22.930
but the same happens.

00:52:22.930 --> 00:52:25.150
It's a parabolic surface.

00:52:25.150 --> 00:52:28.250
And it has to be like this,
because on large scales

00:52:28.250 --> 00:52:30.550
there is a correspondence
equivalence

00:52:30.550 --> 00:52:33.790
between a classical system,
and the quantum system.

00:52:33.790 --> 00:52:36.940
But if you now look a little bit
closer onto the quantum system,

00:52:36.940 --> 00:52:40.120
you will find that
the system is littered

00:52:40.120 --> 00:52:42.660
with tiny little holes.

00:52:42.660 --> 00:52:45.490
Tiny little vortices, so
there's a big difference,

00:52:45.490 --> 00:52:47.740
and I want to
explain that to you.

00:52:47.740 --> 00:52:51.340
The reason is the
magic of matter waves.

00:52:51.340 --> 00:52:54.340
We're not asking particles
to go around, and go around.

00:52:54.340 --> 00:52:56.800
We are asking
waves to go around.

00:52:56.800 --> 00:52:58.930
And due to the laws
of quantum mechanics,

00:52:58.930 --> 00:53:01.690
we have a snake of
waves, and the snake

00:53:01.690 --> 00:53:03.700
has to bite into its tail.

00:53:03.700 --> 00:53:07.710
It has to it form a closed wave.

00:53:07.710 --> 00:53:10.680
So therefore, we have the
quantization condition

00:53:10.680 --> 00:53:14.460
that we need an integer number
of de Broglie wavelengths

00:53:14.460 --> 00:53:17.030
around the circumference.

00:53:17.030 --> 00:53:20.420
So that means now if
you rotate the bucket,

00:53:20.420 --> 00:53:24.110
the classical system
just rigid body rotation

00:53:24.110 --> 00:53:29.500
has a velocity which increases
linearly with distance.

00:53:29.500 --> 00:53:32.650
But the quantum fluid,
the quantum gas,

00:53:32.650 --> 00:53:36.110
cannot do it because it has,
sort of, to make a decision.

00:53:36.110 --> 00:53:39.820
Single matter waves around the
circumference, one, two, three,

00:53:39.820 --> 00:53:43.950
so something has to go in jumps.

00:53:43.950 --> 00:53:47.010
One possibility would be
that we have a region where

00:53:47.010 --> 00:53:48.720
the system is not moving.

00:53:48.720 --> 00:53:50.306
This means single matter waves.

00:53:50.306 --> 00:53:51.680
Here we have one
de Broglie wave.

00:53:51.680 --> 00:53:53.550
Here two, and here three.

00:53:53.550 --> 00:53:55.440
But a little bit more
detailed consideration

00:53:55.440 --> 00:53:58.440
tells us that instead
of forming this kind

00:53:58.440 --> 00:54:01.590
of ring-shaped
geometry, it rather

00:54:01.590 --> 00:54:04.770
the system rather breaks
up into little whirlpools,

00:54:04.770 --> 00:54:06.135
little vortices.

00:54:06.135 --> 00:54:10.090
And Around each vortices
we have one full de Broglie

00:54:10.090 --> 00:54:11.730
wavelengths.

00:54:11.730 --> 00:54:14.660
Now this is a very
universal consequence.

00:54:14.660 --> 00:54:19.280
If you have matter,
which is a single wave.

00:54:19.280 --> 00:54:22.120
And indeed, there is a
very important experiment

00:54:22.120 --> 00:54:24.460
done 20 years ago
at Berkeley where

00:54:24.460 --> 00:54:28.780
this phenomenon was
demonstrated with liquid helium.

00:54:28.780 --> 00:54:33.850
And also, with Bose condensates,
recently a group in Paris

00:54:33.850 --> 00:54:35.670
showed this effect.

00:54:35.670 --> 00:54:37.354
I'm done talking
about recent work,

00:54:37.354 --> 00:54:38.770
so I want to talk
now about things

00:54:38.770 --> 00:54:43.100
which went on in our laboratory
less than a year ago.

00:54:43.100 --> 00:54:45.430
So we try to sort of
go further, and study

00:54:45.430 --> 00:54:47.030
further aspects of it.

00:54:47.030 --> 00:54:48.910
So what we did is,
and this gives you

00:54:48.910 --> 00:54:51.890
sort of a taste what
happens in Building 26.

00:54:51.890 --> 00:54:55.720
Is this tiny little cloud
in a big vacuum chamber,

00:54:55.720 --> 00:54:59.740
and then we use a laser beam,
and we rotate the laser beam

00:54:59.740 --> 00:55:00.990
around the cloud.

00:55:00.990 --> 00:55:04.360
And with the laser beam,
we are spinning up the gas,

00:55:04.360 --> 00:55:07.850
and we're setting
the gas in rotation.

00:55:07.850 --> 00:55:10.910
And then, if you let the gas go.

00:55:10.910 --> 00:55:13.220
If you let the cloud
ballistically expand,

00:55:13.220 --> 00:55:17.180
we can now compare the
cloud without rotation.

00:55:17.180 --> 00:55:20.090
It's just a blob, which expands,
as I've shown you before.

00:55:20.090 --> 00:55:24.750
But with rotation you
observe those whirlpools.

00:55:24.750 --> 00:55:28.110
So this is, again a
very clear manifestation

00:55:28.110 --> 00:55:29.760
of the wave nature of matter.

00:55:29.760 --> 00:55:33.420
Ordinary, standard substances
would not behave like this.

00:55:33.420 --> 00:55:36.330
And again, it's the
most direct observation.

00:55:36.330 --> 00:55:38.340
It's a cloud which rotates.

00:55:38.340 --> 00:55:40.410
You let it expand,
you take a picture,

00:55:40.410 --> 00:55:41.630
and this is the shadow.

00:55:41.630 --> 00:55:42.800
The shadow of this cloud.

00:55:46.160 --> 00:55:49.210
Actually, this is a simulation
by one of my colleagues

00:55:49.210 --> 00:55:54.110
shows that this
structure of whirlpools

00:55:54.110 --> 00:55:57.810
is preserved upon the
expansion of the gas.

00:55:57.810 --> 00:56:01.180
We can directly photograph
the cloud while it is rotating

00:56:01.180 --> 00:56:04.790
because the structures are too
small, but by simply switching

00:56:04.790 --> 00:56:07.220
off the magnetic trap,
and everything expands.

00:56:07.220 --> 00:56:10.550
We get almost, we get
the magnification factor

00:56:10.550 --> 00:56:13.060
almost for free.

00:56:13.060 --> 00:56:16.830
And the faster we rotated
the cloud, the more vortices

00:56:16.830 --> 00:56:19.400
we observed.

00:56:19.400 --> 00:56:21.950
So this is an example
of a vortex lattice,

00:56:21.950 --> 00:56:24.560
which is perfectly regular.

00:56:24.560 --> 00:56:27.090
This is just a more
fancy representation.

00:56:27.090 --> 00:56:29.900
But let's just look at this
very regular vortex lattice.

00:56:29.900 --> 00:56:32.000
With those lines
which guide the eye,

00:56:32.000 --> 00:56:36.570
we see how perfect nature is.

00:56:36.570 --> 00:56:38.870
But it's not always
perfect, and if you'll

00:56:38.870 --> 00:56:40.960
look carefully on
the left-hand side,

00:56:40.960 --> 00:56:42.510
you'll see a lattice defect.

00:56:42.510 --> 00:56:47.490
You see a dislocation,
and extra row of vortices.

00:56:47.490 --> 00:56:48.670
So we have now a system.

00:56:48.670 --> 00:56:51.090
It's a very dilute gas.

00:56:51.090 --> 00:56:53.660
And we can now study the
properties of vortices,

00:56:53.660 --> 00:56:55.530
study their
dynamics, and there's

00:56:55.530 --> 00:56:58.780
a lot of interest in doing that.

00:56:58.780 --> 00:57:01.210
But finally, let me
talk about something

00:57:01.210 --> 00:57:02.830
which is even more recent.

00:57:02.830 --> 00:57:06.760
And some work, which is
currently in progress.

00:57:06.760 --> 00:57:10.430
This is-- okay, I will
tell you in a moment.

00:57:10.430 --> 00:57:14.370
This is related to
optical trapping.

00:57:14.370 --> 00:57:17.610
I told you that our workhorse
is a magnetic container.

00:57:17.610 --> 00:57:20.770
But so well, magnetic
containers are nice.

00:57:20.770 --> 00:57:22.500
They have allowed us
to do all this work,

00:57:22.500 --> 00:57:24.870
but they've also
their limitations.

00:57:24.870 --> 00:57:27.030
Because if you want
to move magnets,

00:57:27.030 --> 00:57:29.100
you may have to move
all the magnets around.

00:57:29.100 --> 00:57:31.770
You can only keep atoms
in spin up states,

00:57:31.770 --> 00:57:33.090
and not spin down states.

00:57:33.090 --> 00:57:36.900
So there are many reasons to
put those Bose condensates

00:57:36.900 --> 00:57:38.820
into other containers.

00:57:38.820 --> 00:57:41.610
And this is a
container, which is not

00:57:41.610 --> 00:57:44.190
using magnetic field,
but electric fields.

00:57:44.190 --> 00:57:47.700
And its electric field
over focused laser beam.

00:57:47.700 --> 00:57:51.090
We call it optical tweezers
because it's really

00:57:51.090 --> 00:57:53.370
like a laser pointer.

00:57:53.370 --> 00:57:56.550
We bring the light to
a focus, and this focus

00:57:56.550 --> 00:58:01.250
sucks atoms into it
just by electric forces.

00:58:01.250 --> 00:58:03.840
So, couple of
years ago we showed

00:58:03.840 --> 00:58:06.720
that we can transfer condensates
from the magnetic trap

00:58:06.720 --> 00:58:09.044
into an optical trap.

00:58:09.044 --> 00:58:13.650
But our piece of pride, and
our latest accomplishment

00:58:13.650 --> 00:58:17.790
is that we are now able
to use this optical trap

00:58:17.790 --> 00:58:20.700
as a transport mechanism
for Bose condensates.

00:58:20.700 --> 00:58:24.180
So by taking, not really
the laser, but the lens,

00:58:24.180 --> 00:58:29.460
and translating it, we can move
the focus by 40 centimeters.

00:58:29.460 --> 00:58:32.880
So our group has now
the unique ability

00:58:32.880 --> 00:58:36.180
to form a condensate
in one vacuum chamber,

00:58:36.180 --> 00:58:40.230
then focus laser light on
it, and translate the focus

00:58:40.230 --> 00:58:42.600
by about 40
centimeters, and carry

00:58:42.600 --> 00:58:44.790
the atoms in a new chamber.

00:58:44.790 --> 00:58:47.760
So we are now able to deliver
condensates with precision

00:58:47.760 --> 00:58:50.010
through pin holes,
and put condensates

00:58:50.010 --> 00:58:51.730
into whole new environments.

00:58:51.730 --> 00:58:56.800
Close to surfaces, into
micro traps, into resonators,

00:58:56.800 --> 00:58:58.200
and there's enormous excitement.

00:58:58.200 --> 00:59:01.980
And let me just show
you one of the results.

00:59:01.980 --> 00:59:04.160
This is 40 centimeters
away from where

00:59:04.160 --> 00:59:06.440
we produced the condensate.

00:59:06.440 --> 00:59:10.340
And here it is, and it is
less than a millimeter away

00:59:10.340 --> 00:59:12.260
from the surface.

00:59:12.260 --> 00:59:15.600
And we think there's a lot of
future of atomic physics in it.

00:59:15.600 --> 00:59:19.680
Some people dream that we
can take atoms-guided matter

00:59:19.680 --> 00:59:24.420
waves on atom chips,
tiny patterns of wires

00:59:24.420 --> 00:59:25.290
on the surface.

00:59:25.290 --> 00:59:28.830
And we guide the atoms around
like in an ordinary chip,

00:59:28.830 --> 00:59:30.820
electrons are guided.

00:59:30.820 --> 00:59:35.960
And this may allow us
to have new geometries,

00:59:35.960 --> 00:59:38.840
to find new properties
of quantum systems.

00:59:38.840 --> 00:59:42.069
But it may also allow us
to build precision sensors

00:59:42.069 --> 00:59:42.610
for rotation.

00:59:46.140 --> 00:59:48.410
But I think this
example will also,

00:59:48.410 --> 00:59:50.550
or the last two
examples demonstrates

00:59:50.550 --> 00:59:52.050
some of my surprises.

00:59:52.050 --> 00:59:53.940
When we were hunting
for the Bose condensate

00:59:53.940 --> 00:59:57.000
we thought we would be so
happy if we would just see it.

00:59:57.000 --> 00:59:58.510
If you would get there.

00:59:58.510 --> 01:00:01.230
But now, we can take
condensates, transport them

01:00:01.230 --> 01:00:03.090
over 40 centimeters.

01:00:03.090 --> 01:00:05.970
We can keep them for a
minute, and do experiments.

01:00:05.970 --> 01:00:08.220
We can spin them
at high rotation,

01:00:08.220 --> 01:00:09.780
and see those vortices.

01:00:09.780 --> 01:00:12.780
So the system is
enormously robust,

01:00:12.780 --> 01:00:17.430
and allows us to branch out
in a large variety of studies.

01:00:17.430 --> 01:00:22.070
So for me, the Bose condensate
is now a new laboratory

01:00:22.070 --> 01:00:23.890
to do all sorts of physics.

01:00:23.890 --> 01:00:27.000
To do condensed metaphysics,
vortex nucleation,

01:00:27.000 --> 01:00:31.380
sound at densities which
are extremely low, that it's

01:00:31.380 --> 01:00:34.740
very easy for theorists
to calculate the effects.

01:00:34.740 --> 01:00:37.380
And what I've done here is
it's more for the experts.

01:00:37.380 --> 01:00:39.630
I've just listed
major developments

01:00:39.630 --> 01:00:43.360
in the field, which have
happened just this year.

01:00:43.360 --> 01:00:45.790
And the other
exciting things, and I

01:00:45.790 --> 01:00:49.520
think there is more to come.

01:00:49.520 --> 01:00:52.870
So, it's a privilege
for scientists

01:00:52.870 --> 01:00:55.330
to be in the middle of
something like this,

01:00:55.330 --> 01:00:58.690
to be able to contribute
to the basic concepts,

01:00:58.690 --> 01:01:03.150
and see the scope of the field,
and see how it's branching out.

01:01:03.150 --> 01:01:04.970
Actually, I don't
have a slide for that.

01:01:04.970 --> 01:01:09.420
But one question, which
almost everybody asks me,

01:01:09.420 --> 01:01:10.420
what is the application?

01:01:10.420 --> 01:01:12.010
What will come out of it?

01:01:12.010 --> 01:01:14.870
Now, my take on that is twofold.

01:01:14.870 --> 01:01:17.880
One is it's
fundamental research,

01:01:17.880 --> 01:01:22.200
and we do fundamental research
to learn about properties

01:01:22.200 --> 01:01:25.320
of nature, to find
properties of matter

01:01:25.320 --> 01:01:28.680
at ultra low temperatures, to
understand macroscopic quantum

01:01:28.680 --> 01:01:29.640
mechanics.

01:01:29.640 --> 01:01:31.650
On the other hand,
what we're doing here

01:01:31.650 --> 01:01:38.150
is we are learning how to
manipulate atoms, the building

01:01:38.150 --> 01:01:41.250
blocks of nature with
unprecedented precision.

01:01:41.250 --> 01:01:43.910
We have quantum
control over the atoms.

01:01:43.910 --> 01:01:46.250
We control their
wave properties,

01:01:46.250 --> 01:01:49.010
and this unprecedented
control over atoms

01:01:49.010 --> 01:01:52.010
may lead to precision
measurements, may

01:01:52.010 --> 01:01:54.640
contribute to nanotechnology.

01:01:54.640 --> 01:01:56.690
Or let me speculate
even further,

01:01:56.690 --> 01:01:59.120
may find uses in
quantum computation.

01:01:59.120 --> 01:02:06.170
But this is at the horizon, and
collaborators and other groups

01:02:06.170 --> 01:02:07.340
are exploring that.

01:02:07.340 --> 01:02:09.800
And future will tell
what is possible.

01:02:09.800 --> 01:02:13.340
But if I look backwards,
usually if something basic is

01:02:13.340 --> 01:02:16.940
done our imagination is
not sufficient to predict

01:02:16.940 --> 01:02:18.930
what it is good for.

01:02:18.930 --> 01:02:21.290
So finally, let me
acknowledge the people I was

01:02:21.290 --> 01:02:23.620
really privileged to work with.

01:02:23.620 --> 01:02:25.620
I think the best
team of students,

01:02:25.620 --> 01:02:27.470
and post-docs in
the whole world.

01:02:27.470 --> 01:02:30.030
This lists the people
who have left the group,

01:02:30.030 --> 01:02:31.680
and contributed to the work.

01:02:31.680 --> 01:02:34.080
But here is also a
long list of people

01:02:34.080 --> 01:02:37.260
who are currently carrying
out this exciting work.

01:02:37.260 --> 01:02:39.600
Usually I show a
group photo, but I

01:02:39.600 --> 01:02:42.660
realize we haven't taken
a group photo recently.

01:02:42.660 --> 01:02:47.400
So may I just ask the people
who are working with me just

01:02:47.400 --> 01:02:48.650
to stand up?

01:02:48.650 --> 01:02:50.800
And it's a live group photo.

01:02:50.800 --> 01:02:53.400
And they are carrying
out this work,

01:02:53.400 --> 01:02:55.974
and they deserve the applause.

01:02:55.974 --> 01:02:59.453
[APPLAUSE]

01:03:30.267 --> 01:03:31.261
Thank you.

01:03:31.261 --> 01:03:32.752
[AUDIENCE LAUGHS]

01:03:32.752 --> 01:03:36.231
